window.BANK = {"title":"Read & Learn","storageKey":"ant1-progress-v2","q":{"1TH0":{"d":"TH","tag":"Anthropology subdisciplines","q":"All of the following are major subdisciplines/branches of anthropology EXCEPT","ok":"Geology","wrong":["Archaeology","Biological anthropology","Linguistic anthropology","Cultural anthropology"],"ex":"The four fields are cultural, biological, archaeology and linguistic. Archaeology IS one, so it can't be the EXCEPT answer.","topic":"Science & method","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH0"},"1TH1":{"d":"TH","tag":"Hypothesis","q":"What are two essential characteristics necessary when formulating a hypothesis?","ok":"Falsifiability and testability","wrong":["Popularity and certainty","Testability and proof","Complexity and consensus","Certainty and falsifiability"],"ex":"A hypothesis must be testable (you can check it with observations or experiments) and falsifiable (some possible result could prove it wrong). Science never proves an idea with certainty.","topic":"Science & method","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH1"},"1AP9":{"d":"AP","tag":"Science in Practice","q":"A scientific hypothesis must meet two criteria: testability and this","ok":"What is falsifiability?","wrong":["What is popularity?","What is certainty?","What is complexity?"],"ex":"A hypothesis must be testable and falsifiable: there must be some result that could prove it wrong.","topic":"Science & method","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP9"},"1AP5":{"d":"AP","tag":"Science in Practice","q":"In the scientific method, the statement of what you predict to see, an educated guess based on observations","ok":"What is a hypothesis?","wrong":["What is a theory?","What is a law?","What is a fact?"],"ex":"A hypothesis is a testable, educated prediction based on observations.","topic":"Science & method","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP5"},"1AP7":{"d":"AP","tag":"Science in Practice","q":"Investigating phenomena by stating a hypothesis, collecting data and testing the hypothesis","ok":"What is the scientific method?","wrong":["What is uniformitarianism?","What is peer review?","What is natural theology?"],"ex":"The scientific method: observe, form a hypothesis, collect data, test it, and revise.","topic":"Science & method","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP7"},"1TH2":{"d":"TH","tag":"Well tested model","q":"A well-tested explanation of nature that has survived repeated attempts to refute it is called","ok":"A theory","wrong":["A hypothesis","A law","A fact"],"ex":"A theory is a well-tested explanation that has survived repeated attempts to disprove it. A hypothesis is a testable prediction, and a law describes a pattern without explaining it.","topic":"Science & method","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH2"},"1AP8":{"d":"AP","tag":"Science in Practice","q":"A well-tested model that has survived repeated tests and refinements; it means something different in non-scientific settings","ok":"What is a theory?","wrong":["What is a hypothesis?","What is a law?","What is a guess?"],"ex":"In science, a theory is a well-tested explanation (like evolution), not a guess as in everyday speech.","topic":"Science & method","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP8"},"1TH3":{"d":"TH","tag":"Hierarchical ladder","q":"The universal hierarchical ladder of all matter and life that framed early understanding of humans' position in the world:","ok":"Great Chain of Being / Scala Naturae","wrong":["Tree of life","Uniformitarianism","Binomial nomenclature"],"ex":"The Great Chain of Being (Scala Naturae) ranked all matter and life on one fixed ladder, from rocks up through plants, animals and humans to the divine, with species never changing.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH3"},"1AP10":{"d":"AP","tag":"A World Before Darwin","q":"The pre-Darwinian belief that all organisms exist on a hierarchical ladder and species were fixed","ok":"What is the Great Chain of Being (Scala Naturae)?","wrong":["What is the tree of life?","What is uniformitarianism?","What is binomial nomenclature?"],"ex":"The Great Chain of Being (Scala Naturae) ranked all matter and life on one fixed ladder, from rocks up through plants, animals and humans to the divine, with species never changing.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP10"},"1AP14":{"d":"AP","tag":"A World Before Darwin","q":"Swedish naturalist who devised the taxonomic hierarchy and binomial nomenclature","ok":"Who is Carolus Linnaeus?","wrong":["Who is Jean-Baptiste Lamarck?","Who is Gregor Mendel?","Who is Charles Lyell?"],"ex":"Carolus Linnaeus (Sweden) built the taxonomic hierarchy (kingdom down to species) and binomial nomenclature, two-part names like Homo sapiens.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP14"},"1TH6":{"d":"TH","tag":"Linnaeus","q":"Which of the following is NOT a contribution of Carolus Linnaeus?","ok":"Presented a heliocentric view of the universe","wrong":["Binomial nomenclature (Genus species)","A hierarchical taxonomic system","Classifying humans with other primates"],"ex":"Heliocentrism is Copernicus.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH6"},"1PP28":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Carolus Linnaeus","q":"Naming our species Homo sapiens (Genus species) is an example of which Linnaeus contribution?","ok":"Binomial nomenclature","wrong":["Uniformitarianism","Natural selection","The Great Chain of Being"],"ex":"Binomial nomenclature gives every species a two-part name, genus + species, like Homo sapiens.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP28"},"1PP29":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Carolus Linnaeus","q":"Carolus Linnaeus was a naturalist from:","ok":"Sweden","wrong":["England","France","Austria"],"ex":"Carolus Linnaeus (Sweden) built the taxonomic hierarchy (kingdom down to species) and binomial nomenclature, two-part names like Homo sapiens.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP29"},"1TH7":{"d":"TH","tag":"Explanatory models","q":"The model stating that geological processes today are the same as in the past, slow and gradual, so the earth must be immensely old:","ok":"Uniformitarianism","wrong":["Catastrophism","Great Chain of Being","Adaptive radiation"],"ex":"Uniformitarianism (Hutton, then Lyell): the slow processes we see today, like erosion and sedimentation, also shaped the earth in the past, so the earth must be very old. Catastrophism instead blamed sudden disasters.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH7"},"1AP18":{"d":"AP","tag":"Darwin's Evidence","q":"Geological processes seen today operated in the past at a gradual pace, permitting an Earth older than 6,000 years","ok":"What is uniformitarianism?","wrong":["What is catastrophism?","What is the Great Chain of Being?","What is adaptive radiation?"],"ex":"Uniformitarianism (Hutton, then Lyell): the slow processes we see today, like erosion and sedimentation, also shaped the earth in the past, so the earth must be very old. Catastrophism instead blamed sudden disasters.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP18"},"1AP11":{"d":"AP","tag":"A World Before Darwin","q":"Wrote Principles of Geology describing uniformitarianism","ok":"Who is Charles Lyell?","wrong":["Who is Thomas Malthus?","Who is Jean-Baptiste Lamarck?","Who is Carolus Linnaeus?"],"ex":"Charles Lyell's Principles of Geology popularized uniformitarianism; Darwin read it on the Beagle.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP11"},"1PP23":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Charles Lyell","q":"Why did Lyell's uniformitarianism matter for Darwin's theory?","ok":"It meant the earth was immensely old, giving time for slow, gradual change","wrong":["It proved species were fixed","It explained where variation comes from","It showed traits blend in offspring"],"ex":"Gradual processes need an earth far older than 6,000 years.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP23"},"1TH4":{"d":"TH","tag":"Darwin's inspiration","q":"The English economist who inspired Darwin with his argument that overpopulation drives competition for resources, a \"struggle for existence\":","ok":"Thomas Malthus","wrong":["Charles Lyell","Adam Smith","Alfred Russel Wallace","Jean-Baptiste Lamarck"],"ex":"You left this blank on Top Hat. Malthus wrote An Essay on the Principle of Population.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH4"},"1AP12":{"d":"AP","tag":"A World Before Darwin","q":"This English economist wrote An Essay on the Principle of Population, which inspired Darwin","ok":"Who is Thomas Malthus?","wrong":["Who is Charles Lyell?","Who is Adam Smith?","Who is Alfred Russel Wallace?"],"ex":"Thomas Malthus argued that populations grow faster than their food supply, causing a struggle for existence. That helped Darwin see natural selection.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP12"},"1PP25":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Thomas Malthus","q":"Malthus argued that overpopulation drives competition for resources. Darwin took this as the idea of a:","ok":"Struggle for existence","wrong":["Great Chain of Being","Tree of life","Scala Naturae"],"ex":"Malthus's idea that overpopulation leads to competition became Darwin's \"struggle for existence\".","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP25"},"1PP35":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Match them","q":"Which person is matched with the right book?","ok":"Malthus: An Essay on the Principle of Population","wrong":["Lyell: On the Origin of Species","Darwin: Principles of Geology","Linnaeus: An Essay on the Principle of Population"],"ex":"Lyell wrote Principles of Geology; Darwin wrote On the Origin of Species.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP35"},"1TH5":{"d":"TH","tag":"Lamarck","q":"Which best describes Lamarck's theory of inheritance of acquired characteristics?","ok":"Traits acquired by an organism during its lifetime can be passed on to its offspring.","wrong":["Species are fixed and never change.","Individuals with favorable variations leave more offspring.","Traits from both parents blend together in offspring."],"ex":"Lamarck proposed the inheritance of acquired characteristics: traits gained during a lifetime (like a giraffe stretching its neck) pass to offspring. It was the first explanation of how change happens, but genetics showed acquired traits aren't inherited.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH5"},"1AP13":{"d":"AP","tag":"A World Before Darwin","q":"This naturalist formulated the Inheritance of Acquired Characteristics, the first explanation for how evolutionary change occurred","ok":"Who is Jean-Baptiste Lamarck?","wrong":["Who is Carolus Linnaeus?","Who is Charles Lyell?","Who is Charles Darwin?"],"ex":"Lamarck proposed the inheritance of acquired characteristics: traits gained during a lifetime (like a giraffe stretching its neck) pass to offspring. It was the first explanation of how change happens, but genetics showed acquired traits aren't inherited.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP13"},"1PP26":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Jean-Baptiste Lamarck","q":"A giraffe stretches its neck to reach leaves and passes the longer neck on to its offspring. Whose idea is this?","ok":"Jean-Baptiste Lamarck","wrong":["Charles Darwin","Thomas Malthus","Gregor Mendel"],"ex":"Inheritance of acquired characteristics. It's wrong, but it was the first proposed mechanism.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP26"},"1PP27":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Jean-Baptiste Lamarck","q":"Which pre-Darwin thinker gave the first explanation for HOW evolutionary change occurs?","ok":"Jean-Baptiste Lamarck","wrong":["Carolus Linnaeus","Charles Lyell","Thomas Malthus"],"ex":"Lamarck proposed the inheritance of acquired characteristics: traits gained during a lifetime (like a giraffe stretching its neck) pass to offspring. It was the first explanation of how change happens, but genetics showed acquired traits aren't inherited.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP27"},"1PP34":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Match them","q":"Which pairing is WRONG?","ok":"Lamarck: natural selection","wrong":["Lyell: uniformitarianism","Malthus: population and struggle for existence","Linnaeus: taxonomy and binomial nomenclature"],"ex":"Lamarck = inheritance of acquired characteristics. Natural selection = Darwin and Wallace.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP34"},"1AC1":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 2","q":"Charles Darwin was first sent off to Edinburgh, Scotland in order","ok":"to study medicine","wrong":["to enroll in a natural sciences major","to begin courses in evolutionary biology","to study poetry","to become a gentleman scholar"],"ex":"His father sent him to Edinburgh to study medicine, but Darwin couldn't stand surgery and left.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC1"},"1AC2":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 3","q":"Darwin's role on the HMS Beagle was to","ok":"serve as a conversationalist to the captain","wrong":["provide spiritual guidance to the passengers","navigate the boat","clean the vessel","provide medical assistance to the crew"],"ex":"Captain FitzRoy wanted a gentleman companion to talk to on the long voyage. That was Darwin's role.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC2"},"1AC3":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 4","q":"When the HMS Beagle landed in Argentina, what major discovery did Darwin uncover?","ok":"Fossils of large extinct mammals","wrong":["13 species of finches with different beaks","Fossils of early pre-humans","A previously uncontacted indigenous population","No living land mammals"],"ex":"In Argentina Darwin found fossils of giant extinct mammals, like giant ground sloths, that resembled living species. That suggested species change over time.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC3"},"1AC4":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 5","q":"The islands off the coast of Ecuador that were among Darwin's most important stops:","ok":"Galápagos Islands","wrong":["Canary Islands","Falkland Islands","Azores"],"ex":"Spelling counts on the activity.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC4"},"1AC5":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 6","q":"What was so remarkable about Darwin's finches?","ok":"They were variations of a single type: slightly different species on different islands","wrong":["They all belonged to a single species","They lived only where tortoises didn't","They lived only where penguins didn't","They descended from the giant mammal fossils in Argentina"],"ex":"The finches were closely related but separate species on different islands, with beaks suited to local foods: one ancestor diversifying.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC5"},"1AC6":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 7","q":"From his discoveries on the HMS Beagle, Darwin amassed evidence that","ok":"species changed over time","wrong":["he suffered from sea-sickness","the captain was not a good conversationalist","species were created perfectly and could not change","early pre-humans roamed South America"],"ex":"Fossils, finches and other observations convinced Darwin that species change over time instead of being fixed.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC6"},"1TH8":{"d":"TH","tag":"Co-discovery","q":"Another young British naturalist reached the same conclusion as Darwin, that natural selection drives evolutionary change, at about the same time:","ok":"Alfred Russel Wallace","wrong":["Thomas Malthus","Charles Lyell","Gregor Mendel"],"ex":"Alfred Russel Wallace reached natural selection on his own; his 1858 letter pushed Darwin to publish. Malthus inspired both men, Lyell was a geologist, and Mendel worked on heredity.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH8"},"1AP6":{"d":"AP","tag":"Science in Practice","q":"Darwin arrived at natural selection around the same time as this scientist, the \"co-discoverer\" of natural selection","ok":"Who is Alfred Russel Wallace?","wrong":["Who is Thomas Malthus?","Who is Charles Lyell?","Who is Gregor Mendel?"],"ex":"Alfred Russel Wallace reached natural selection on his own; his 1858 letter pushed Darwin to publish. Malthus inspired both men, Lyell was a geologist, and Mendel worked on heredity.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP6"},"1PP20":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Charles Darwin","q":"Who wrote On the Origin of Species, laying out evolution by natural selection?","ok":"Charles Darwin","wrong":["Alfred Russel Wallace","Charles Lyell","Jean-Baptiste Lamarck"],"ex":"Charles Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP20"},"1PP21":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Darwin & Wallace","q":"Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace independently arrived at the same mechanism for evolutionary change:","ok":"Natural selection","wrong":["Inheritance of acquired characteristics","Uniformitarianism","Blending inheritance"],"ex":"That's why Wallace is called the co-discoverer of natural selection.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP21"},"1AC8":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 9","q":"Darwin's \"tree of life\" suggests that","ok":"all living and extinct species are connected, some closely, others distantly","wrong":["trees are more closely related to humans than other organisms","all organisms begin life as trees","there are no connections between most organisms","trees are the only organisms worth studying"],"ex":"The tree of life shows every species, living or extinct, branching from common ancestors. Close relatives sit on nearby branches.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC8"},"1AP33":{"d":"AP","tag":"May the Force Be With You","q":"Alleles linked to better survival and reproduction increase in frequency in the next generation","ok":"What is natural selection?","wrong":["What is genetic drift?","What is mutation?","What is gene flow?"],"ex":"Natural selection: alleles that help survival and reproduction become more common over the generations.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP33"},"1TH12":{"d":"TH","tag":"Fitness","q":"Which lizard has the highest fitness? A: very healthy, 19 surviving offspring · B: mated with many, 28 · C: dark and very quick, 22 · D: largest territory, 26","ok":"Lizard B","wrong":["Lizard A","Lizard C","Lizard D"],"ex":"Fitness = number of offspring surviving to adulthood.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH12"},"1AP27":{"d":"AP","tag":"In Evo Terms","q":"Of lizards A (healthy, 19 surviving offspring), B (mated with many, 28), C (dark and quick, 22) and D (largest territory, 26), the one with the greatest fitness","ok":"What is lizard B?","wrong":["What is lizard A?","What is lizard C?","What is lizard D?"],"ex":"Fitness = offspring surviving to adulthood.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP27"},"1TH14":{"d":"TH","tag":"How natural selection works","q":"Natural selection operates on ______, while evolution occurs at the ______ level.","ok":"individuals; population","wrong":["populations; individual","genes; species","species; gene"],"ex":"Selection acts on individuals: who survives and reproduces. Evolution is the change in allele frequencies in a population over generations.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH14"},"1TH11":{"d":"TH","tag":"Misconceptions","q":"Which of these is NOT a misconception about evolution (i.e., which is TRUE)?","ok":"Individuals with favorable variations are more likely to leave offspring.","wrong":["Individuals evolve during their lifetime.","Evolution produces perfect organisms.","Organisms evolve because they need to."],"ex":"This is how natural selection works: individuals with favorable variations survive and reproduce more. The rest are misconceptions. Individuals don't evolve, evolution doesn't make perfect organisms, and it has no goals or needs.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH11"},"1AP26":{"d":"AP","tag":"In Evo Terms","q":"True or false: organisms have many traits that are not perfectly structured but function well enough to give a competitive edge","ok":"What is TRUE?","wrong":["What is FALSE?"],"ex":"TRUE. Evolution produces \"good enough\", not perfect: a trait only has to work better than the alternatives.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP26"},"1AC10":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 11","q":"Rock pocket mice living on dark rocks in Arizona's Pinacate Desert avoid predators because","ok":"they are camouflaged by their dark fur","wrong":["they offer predators Snickers bars","they live under the rocks","they have light fur and are visible targets","they can outrun any predator"],"ex":"On dark lava rock, dark-furred mice are hard for predators to see, so they survive and reproduce more. That's natural selection.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC10"},"1AP19":{"d":"AP","tag":"Darwin's Evidence","q":"Structures similar across species, indicating common ancestry even when they serve different functions","ok":"What are homologous structures?","wrong":["What are vestigial structures?","What are analogous structures?","What are transitional structures?"],"ex":"Homologous structures share an underlying form from a common ancestor even when they do different jobs, like a human arm, bat wing and whale flipper. Analogous structures do similar jobs but evolved separately.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP19"},"1AP15":{"d":"AP","tag":"Darwin's Evidence","q":"Functionless structures that Darwin argued were evidence of an evolutionary past","ok":"What are vestigial structures?","wrong":["What are homologous structures?","What are analogous structures?","What are transitional forms?"],"ex":"Vestigial structures, like the human tailbone, have lost their original function but show an evolutionary past.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP15"},"1PP33":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Charles Darwin","q":"Darwin argued that functionless structures were evidence of an evolutionary past. These are:","ok":"Vestigial structures","wrong":["Homologous structures","Analogous structures","Transitional forms"],"ex":"Vestigial structures, like the human tailbone, have lost their original function but show an evolutionary past.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP33"},"1AC7":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 8","q":"Slits around the neck of fish embryos develop into gills; the same slits in humans develop into","ok":"bones of the inner ear","wrong":["earlobes","bones of the shoulders","bones in the arms","the collar bone"],"ex":"The same embryonic structures become gills in fish but ear bones in humans, a sign of shared ancestry (homology).","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC7"},"1AC9":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 10","q":"The Whippet was bred by combining traits from other dogs to chase rabbits. Choosing individual traits to change a breed is","ok":"artificial selection","wrong":["natural selection","frankensteinism","Lamarckianism","evolutionary change"],"ex":"Artificial selection: humans choose which individuals breed to get the traits they want, as with dog breeds.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC9"},"1TH13":{"d":"TH","tag":"Antibiotics","q":"Additional sources of antibiotics, including doses given to livestock, play a role in causing resistant bacteria to evolve.","ok":"TRUE","wrong":["FALSE"],"ex":"TRUE. Every use of antibiotics, including in livestock, kills susceptible bacteria and leaves the resistant ones to multiply, so resistance spreads by natural selection.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH13"},"1TH9":{"d":"TH","tag":"Human ancestors","q":"After the dinosaurs died out (~65 mya), mammal species boomed to fill the ecological niches dinosaurs once occupied. This process is:","ok":"Adaptive radiation","wrong":["Population bottleneck","Founder effect","Sexual selection"],"ex":"Adaptive radiation: one ancestral group quickly diversifies into many species that fill open niches. Mammals did this after the dinosaurs died out.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH9"},"1AP17":{"d":"AP","tag":"Darwin's Evidence","q":"On the Galápagos, one founding finch species diversifying into multiple species","ok":"What is adaptive radiation?","wrong":["What is the founder effect?","What is a population bottleneck?","What is artificial selection?"],"ex":"Adaptive radiation: one ancestral group quickly diversifies into many species that fill open niches. Darwin's finches are the classic example.","topic":"Darwin & natural selection","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP17"},"1AC0":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 1","q":"Darwin acknowledged important holes in his theory. What is the \"hole\" described in the film?","ok":"He couldn't explain where variation in biological organisms came from","wrong":["He didn't understand humans and chimpanzees","He didn't understand extinct vs. living organisms that resembled each other","He didn't understand dog breeding","He didn't understand pigeon breeding"],"ex":"Darwin saw that variation existed but couldn't explain where it came from or how it was inherited. Genetics answered that later.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC0"},"1TH10":{"d":"TH","tag":"Darwin's evidence","q":"Which line of evidence was NOT available to Darwin and so not included in Origin of Species?","ok":"Genetics","wrong":["Fossils","Artificial selection","Biogeography","Comparative anatomy"],"ex":"Darwin didn't know how traits are inherited: Mendel's work wasn't widely known until 1900, and DNA came much later. Fossils, artificial selection, biogeography and comparative anatomy were all in Origin of Species.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH10"},"1AP16":{"d":"AP","tag":"Darwin's Evidence","q":"The popular 19th-century hypothesis that blocked acceptance of Mendel; under it, variation is washed away each generation","ok":"What is blending inheritance?","wrong":["What is particulate inheritance?","What is codominance?","What is acquired inheritance?"],"ex":"Blending inheritance said offspring are an average of their parents, so variation would be watered down each generation. Mendel showed traits pass on as separate units (particulate inheritance).","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP16"},"1PP30":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Gregor Mendel","q":"Blending inheritance was a barrier to the acceptance of whose principles of inheritance?","ok":"Gregor Mendel","wrong":["Charles Lyell","Thomas Malthus","Carolus Linnaeus"],"ex":"Under blending, variation would wash away each generation; Mendel showed traits are passed on as separate units.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP30"},"1TH16":{"d":"TH","tag":"Prokaryote and eukaryote","q":"One primary way in which prokaryotes differ from eukaryotes is:","ok":"Prokaryotes have no nucleus","wrong":["Prokaryotes have no hereditary material","Prokaryotes have no cell membrane","Prokaryotes are always multicellular"],"ex":"You missed this one. Prokaryotes DO have DNA; it just floats in the cytoplasm with no nucleus around it.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH16"},"1AC30":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 31","q":"The structure that separates DNA from the rest of the cell, the \"brain\" of the cell:","ok":"the nucleus","wrong":["cytoplasm","gametes","mitochondria","ribosomes"],"ex":"The nucleus holds the DNA and separates it from the rest of the cell (the cytoplasm).","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC30"},"1AP1":{"d":"AP","tag":"The Wacky World of DNA","q":"DNA replication occurs in this part of the cell","ok":"What is the nucleus?","wrong":["What is the cytoplasm?","What is the ribosome?","What is the mitochondria?"],"ex":"DNA is copied (replicated) in the nucleus, where it's kept.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP1"},"1TH17":{"d":"TH","tag":"Genetic information","q":"Genetic information is transmitted from parent to offspring via which cells?","ok":"Gametes","wrong":["Somatic cells","Red blood cells","Neurons"],"ex":"Gametes (sperm and eggs) carry DNA to the next generation. Somatic (body) cells, like blood cells and neurons, aren't passed on.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH17"},"1AP32":{"d":"AP","tag":"May the Force Be With You","q":"Mutations that arise in these cells have important implications for evolution","ok":"What are gametes?","wrong":["What are somatic cells?","What are skin cells?","What are neurons?"],"ex":"Only mutations in gametes (sperm and eggs) reach the next generation, so they're the ones that matter for evolution.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP32"},"1TH19":{"d":"TH","tag":"Sugar","q":"What sugar makes up the backbone of DNA?","ok":"Deoxyribose","wrong":["Ribose","Glucose","Fructose"],"ex":"DNA is deoxyribonucleic acid, so its sugar is deoxyribose. RNA uses ribose.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH19"},"1AC32":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 33","q":"The three components of a single DNA nucleotide are","ok":"a nitrogenous base, phosphate, and deoxyribose sugar","wrong":["a hydrogen bond, deoxyribose sugar, and phosphate","a hydrogen bond, ribose sugar, and phosphate","a nitrogenous base, phosphate, and a peptide bond","a phosphate, a hydrogen bond, and a nitrogenous base"],"ex":"Each nucleotide is a phosphate, a deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base. Hydrogen bonds join the bases of the two strands; they aren't part of one nucleotide.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC32"},"1AC33":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 34","q":"Base-pairing rules: adenine (A) always bonds with ______, and cytosine (C) always bonds with ______.","ok":"thymine; guanine","wrong":["guanine; thymine","uracil; guanine","cytosine; adenine"],"ex":"A pairs with T, and C pairs with G. (In RNA, A pairs with U.)","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC33"},"1AC11":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 12","q":"The DNA helix is made of four bases, represented by the letters:","ok":"G, A, T, C","wrong":["A, L, D, N","D, N, A, S","G, A, T, R"],"ex":"DNA's four bases are guanine, adenine, thymine and cytosine: G, A, T, C.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC11"},"1AC12":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 13","q":"When examining DNA, what accounts for the differences across all living creatures?","ok":"All organisms have the same letters, but differences in letter sequence","wrong":["Some organisms have more letters than others","Some organisms have no letters in their DNA","Some organisms do not have DNA","Different organisms use different letters"],"ex":"All life uses the same four letters; what differs is their order (sequence).","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC12"},"1AC38":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 39","q":"What is the only characteristic that differs between the plant, mammal and bacterium DNA segments?","ok":"the sequence (order) of bases","wrong":["the sequence of the sugar-phosphate backbone","everything is different","bacteria and plants do not have DNA"],"ex":"Only the order of the bases differs, and that order carries the genetic information.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC38"},"1AC36":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 37","q":"Is the sequence (order) of bases the same in each type of DNA?","ok":"False","wrong":["True"],"ex":"The order of bases is the only thing that differs.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC36"},"1TH20":{"d":"TH","tag":"RNA vs DNA","q":"Which is NOT a difference between DNA and RNA?","ok":"RNA contains bases including adenine, cytosine and guanine","wrong":["DNA has thymine; RNA has uracil","DNA has deoxyribose; RNA has ribose","DNA is double-stranded; RNA is single-stranded"],"ex":"DNA has A, C and G too, so that's a similarity.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH20"},"1AP0":{"d":"AP","tag":"The Wacky World of DNA","q":"Of adenine, thymine, guanine and cytosine, the base that is not found in RNA","ok":"What is thymine?","wrong":["What is adenine?","What is guanine?","What is cytosine?"],"ex":"RNA uses uracil instead.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP0"},"1TH21":{"d":"TH","tag":"DNA function","q":"What process is depicted: DNA → mRNA","ok":"Transcription","wrong":["Translation","Replication","Mutation"],"ex":"Transcription copies a gene's DNA into mRNA, in the nucleus. Translation then reads the mRNA to build a protein.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH21"},"1AC42":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 43","q":"The process in which mRNA copies the DNA template to carry it to the ribosome is called","ok":"transcription","wrong":["translation","replication","transformation"],"ex":"Spelling counts on the activity.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC42"},"1AC51":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 45 · 8/8","q":"Match: Where transcription occurs","ok":"In the nucleus","wrong":["In the cytoplasm","Hydrogen bond","Deoxyribose"],"ex":"Transcription happens in the nucleus, where the DNA is.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC51"},"1AC45":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 45 · 2/8","q":"Match: Where translation occurs","ok":"In the cytoplasm","wrong":["In the nucleus","Deoxyribose","Peptide bond"],"ex":"Translation happens at ribosomes, in the cytoplasm.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC45"},"1AC50":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 45 · 7/8","q":"Match: Codon","ok":"Series of three-base sequences in mRNA","wrong":["Series of three-base sequences in tRNA","Chain of amino acids","Peptide bond"],"ex":"A codon is a three-base sequence on mRNA that codes for one amino acid.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC50"},"1AC47":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 45 · 4/8","q":"Match: Anticodon","ok":"Series of three-base sequences in tRNA","wrong":["Series of three-base sequences in mRNA","Chain of amino acids","Hydrogen bond"],"ex":"An anticodon is the three-base sequence on tRNA that matches an mRNA codon.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC47"},"1AC46":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 45 · 3/8","q":"Match: Binds amino acids together to form a protein","ok":"Peptide bond","wrong":["Hydrogen bond","Deoxyribose","Chain of amino acids"],"ex":"Peptide bonds join amino acids into a chain. Hydrogen bonds are what hold DNA's two strands together.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC46"},"1AC44":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 45 · 1/8","q":"Match: Polypeptide","ok":"Chain of amino acids","wrong":["Series of three-base sequences in mRNA","Peptide bond","Hydrogen bond"],"ex":"A polypeptide is a chain of amino acids linked by peptide bonds; it folds into a protein.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC44"},"1AC39":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 40","q":"DNA template strand <span class=seq>TCA GCC TAA</span>. The transcribed mRNA codons are:","ok":"<span class=seq>AGU CGG AUU</span>","wrong":["<span class=seq>UGT CGG UTT</span>","<span class=seq>AGT CGG ATT</span>","<span class=seq>AUT CUU ATT</span>","<span class=seq>TCA GCC TAA</span>"],"ex":"Complement each base and use U in place of T.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC39"},"1AC40":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 41","q":"DNA template strand <span class=seq>TCA GCC TAA</span>. The tRNA anticodons are:","ok":"<span class=seq>UCA GCC UAA</span>","wrong":["<span class=seq>AGU CGG AUU</span>","<span class=seq>AGT CGG ATT</span>","<span class=seq>AUT CGG ATT</span>","<span class=seq>TCA GCC TAA</span>"],"ex":"Anticodons pair with mRNA, so they match the template with U for T.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC40"},"1AC41":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 42","q":"Using the codon table, DNA template <span class=seq>TCA GCC TAA</span> codes for which amino acids?","ok":"serine – arginine – isoleucine","wrong":["serine – alanine – stop","isoleucine – arginine – serine","serine – stop – isoleucine","serine – arginine – stop"],"ex":"Translate the mRNA (AGU CGG AUU), not the DNA.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC41"},"1SQ0":{"d":"SQ","tag":"From Anthro-pardy · $400","q":"One side of a DNA strand has <span class=seq>AAG TTC CTT</span>. The other strand contains these bases:","ok":"<span class=seq>TTC AAG GAA</span>","wrong":["<span class=seq>UUC AAG GAA</span>","<span class=seq>AAG TTC CTT</span>","<span class=seq>TTG AAC GAA</span>"],"ex":"DNA pairs A↔T and G↔C. No U in DNA.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"SQ0"},"1SQ8":{"d":"SQ","tag":"Practice · Complementary strand","q":"One DNA strand reads <span class=seq>TCA TAC ATC</span>. What is the complementary DNA strand?","ok":"<span class=seq>AGT ATG TAG</span>","wrong":["<span class=seq>AGU AUG UAG</span>","<span class=seq>TCA TAC ATC</span>","<span class=seq>GAT GTA TGA</span>"],"ex":"Pair A↔T and G↔C: TCA TAC ATC → AGT ATG TAG. DNA never contains U.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"SQ8"},"1AP4":{"d":"AP","tag":"The Wacky World of DNA","q":"A template strand of protein-coding DNA has <span class=seq>GTA AAG TAG</span>. The corresponding mRNA codons","ok":"What is <span class=seq>CAU UUC AUC</span>?","wrong":["What is <span class=seq>CAT TTC ATC</span>?","What is <span class=seq>GUA AAG UAG</span>?","What is <span class=seq>CAU UUC AUG</span>?"],"ex":"Pair each base and use U instead of T (G → C, T → A, A → U): GTA AAG TAG becomes CAU UUC AUC.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP4"},"1SQ9":{"d":"SQ","tag":"Practice · Transcription","q":"DNA template strand <span class=seq>CCA ATA GAC</span>. What mRNA codons are transcribed?","ok":"<span class=seq>GGU UAU CUG</span>","wrong":["<span class=seq>GGT TAT CTG</span>","<span class=seq>CCA AUA GAC</span>","<span class=seq>CCA ATA GAC</span>"],"ex":"Complement each base and use U instead of T: CCA ATA GAC → GGU UAU CUG.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"SQ9"},"1SQ10":{"d":"SQ","tag":"Practice · Anticodons","q":"DNA template strand <span class=seq>CTA CCA CTC</span>. What are the tRNA anticodons?","ok":"<span class=seq>CUA CCA CUC</span>","wrong":["<span class=seq>GAU GGU GAG</span>","<span class=seq>CTA CCA CTC</span>","<span class=seq>GAT GGT GAG</span>"],"ex":"mRNA is GAU GGU GAG; anticodons pair with it, giving CUA CCA CUC (the template with U for T).","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"SQ10"},"1SQ11":{"d":"SQ","tag":"Practice · Codons to anticodons","q":"mRNA codons <span class=seq>ACA CUU ACC</span>. Which tRNA anticodons align with them?","ok":"<span class=seq>UGU GAA UGG</span>","wrong":["<span class=seq>TGT GAA TGG</span>","<span class=seq>ACA CUU ACC</span>","<span class=seq>GGU AAG UGU</span>"],"ex":"Pair A↔U and G↔C: ACA CUU ACC → UGU GAA UGG.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"SQ11"},"1SQ13":{"d":"SQ","tag":"Practice · Translation from mRNA","q":"Using the genetic code, which amino acids do the mRNA codons <span class=seq>GGU CUG UCG</span> code for?","ok":"glycine – leucine – serine","wrong":["proline – aspartic acid – serine","alanine – valine – tryptophan","glycine – valine – serine"],"ex":"Read each codon in the table: GGU CUG UCG → glycine – leucine – serine.","topic":"Protein synthesis & sequences","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"SQ13"},"1TH28":{"d":"TH","tag":"Gene quantity","q":"The number of genes humans have is approximately","ok":"20,000–23,000","wrong":["About 1,000","About 100,000","About 1 million"],"ex":"The film used ~23,000.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH28"},"1AC17":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 18","q":"What percentage of our genome codes for proteins?","ok":"2%","wrong":["98%","100%","22%","50%"],"ex":"Only about 2% of the human genome codes for proteins; much of the rest helps regulate genes.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC17"},"1AC18":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 19","q":"A genetic ______ turns genes on and off (the fruit fly spotted vs. unspotted wings experiment).","ok":"switch","wrong":["mutation","codon","allele"],"ex":"Genetic switches turn genes on or off in particular places and times. The wing spots appear only where the switch is on.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC18"},"1AC19":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 20","q":"Stickleback fish losing their spikes is controlled by the same genetic switch responsible for","ok":"the loss of hindlimbs in manatees and whales","wrong":["larger brains in humans","the loss of light fur in rock pocket mice","beak differences in Galápagos finches","spots on fruit fly wings"],"ex":"The same kind of genetic switch that removed the sticklebacks' pelvic spines is linked to the loss of hindlimbs in manatees and whales: the same toolkit, reused.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC19"},"1AC20":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 21","q":"Differences in beak size and shape among Galápagos finches result from","ok":"the same genes in all finches, turned on and off at different times","wrong":["the paintbrush gene","genetic mutations","different genes for each beak shape","use and disuse"],"ex":"All finches share the same beak genes. The differences come from when, and how strongly, those genes are switched on during development.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC20"},"1TH29":{"d":"TH","tag":"Genes","q":"A highly conserved group of regulatory genes that control segmental patterning across invertebrate and vertebrate bodies:","ok":"HOX genes","wrong":["Paintbrush genes","Junk DNA","Velvet worm genes"],"ex":"HOX genes are highly conserved regulatory genes that lay out the head-to-tail body plan in animals from flies to humans.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH29"},"1AC23":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 24","q":"______ are body-plan genes that are highly conserved (unchanged ~600 million years, found in all complex animals).","ok":"HOX genes","wrong":["Velvet worm genes","Mutations","Paintbrush genes"],"ex":"HOX genes are body-plan genes conserved across complex animals for about 600 million years.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC23"},"1AC16":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 17","q":"According to the film, ______ genes determine where the head and limbs go and whether limbs become arms, legs or wings.","ok":"body-plan","wrong":["junk DNA","master","paintbrush","Icarus"],"ex":"Body-plan (HOX) genes decide where the head, body segments and limbs develop.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC16"},"1AC21":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 22","q":"The 375-million-year-old Tiktaalik is a perfect transitional form because","ok":"it has the body of a fish but an arm-like fin like every four-limbed animal","wrong":["it has a fish body and a human-size brain","it has a fish body and bird wings","it has a human body and a fish-size brain","it has a chimpanzee body and an arm-like fin"],"ex":"Tiktaalik has a fish body, gills and scales, but its fin has arm-like bones (shoulder, elbow, wrist). That makes it a transitional form between fish and four-limbed animals.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC21"},"1AC22":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 23","q":"With no DNA from Tiktaalik, a living relative was used to study body-plan genes. This fish is the","ok":"paddlefish","wrong":["sea bass","cuttlefish","salmon","Tiktaalikfish"],"ex":"With no Tiktaalik DNA, scientists studied the paddlefish, a primitive living fish, to see how fin and limb genes work.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC22"},"1AC24":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 25","q":"According to Dr. Katie Pollard, human and chimpanzee DNA are","ok":"approximately 99% identical","wrong":["exactly the same","not alike in any way","only 2% identical","approximately 50% identical"],"ex":"Humans and chimps share about 99% of their DNA. The small differences, many of them in gene switches, matter a lot.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC24"},"1PP31":{"d":"PP","tag":"Practice · Dr. Katie Pollard","q":"In the film, Dr. Katie Pollard's research compares the DNA of:","ok":"Humans and chimpanzees","wrong":["Finches on different islands","Stickleback fish and manatees","Rock pocket mice"],"ex":"Dr. Katie Pollard compares human and chimpanzee DNA to find what makes us human, such as the HAR1 gene.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"PP31"},"1AC29":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 30","q":"Per Dr. Pollard, about how many mutations separate humans from chimps in the gene for cortex development?","ok":"18","wrong":["2","54","23,000"],"ex":"In HAR1, a gene active in building the cortex, 18 changes separate humans from chimps, while chimps and chickens differ by only 2.","topic":"Scientists & thinkers","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC29"},"1AC28":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 29","q":"The ______ is the part of the brain vital for language, music and mathematics.","ok":"cortex","wrong":["cerebellum","brainstem","hippocampus"],"ex":"Spelling counts on the activity.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC28"},"1AC27":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 28","q":"Which part of the human body changed the most during human evolution?","ok":"The human brain","wrong":["Human feet","Human hands","Human jaw structure","Human lack of body hair"],"ex":"The brain changed the most: human brains are about three times the size of a chimpanzee's.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC27"},"1AC25":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 26","q":"Skull plates in chimpanzees, gorillas and orangutans fuse at about ___ years old, limiting brain growth.","ok":"3–4","wrong":["1","30","35–40"],"ex":"Human skull plates stay open far longer.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC25"},"1AC26":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 27","q":"A rare disorder called ______ causes children to be born with brains half the normal size.","ok":"microcephaly","wrong":["megacephaly","minicephaly","muscular dystrophy"],"ex":"Microcephaly (\"small head\") gives children much smaller brains; the genes involved are clues to how the human brain grew.","topic":"Cells, DNA & genes","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC26"},"1TH23":{"d":"TH","tag":"Chromosome number","q":"Typically, humans have ___ pairs of autosomes and ___ pair(s) of sex chromosomes.","ok":"22; 1","wrong":["23; 1","46; 2","22; 2"],"ex":"22 + 1 = 23 pairs = 46 chromosomes.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH23"},"1AP20":{"d":"AP","tag":"Cells & Genes, Oh My","q":"This cell division occurs only in somatic cells; each daughter cell is exactly like the parent cell","ok":"What is mitosis?","wrong":["What is meiosis?","What is fertilization?","What is recombination?"],"ex":"Mitosis divides body (somatic) cells into two identical copies. Meiosis makes gametes with half the chromosomes.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP20"},"1TH25":{"d":"TH","tag":"Gametes vs somatic cells","q":"Human gametes typically have 23 chromosomes and so are referred to as having a ______ number.","ok":"Haploid","wrong":["Diploid","Triploid","Polyploid"],"ex":"Somatic cells (46) are diploid.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH25"},"1TH24":{"d":"TH","tag":"Genetic shuffling","q":"Recombinant genes (genetic shuffling) arise during this process:","ok":"Meiosis","wrong":["Mitosis","Transcription","Translation"],"ex":"Crossing over happens in meiosis, when gametes form, and it shuffles alleles into new combinations. Mitosis makes identical copies.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH24"},"1TH26":{"d":"TH","tag":"Siblings and genetics","q":"The reason siblings receive different combinations of parental traits is best explained by","ok":"Recombination (crossing over) during meiosis","wrong":["Mitosis","Pleiotropy","Linkage"],"ex":"Crossing over (recombination) in meiosis swaps pieces between homologous chromosomes, so each sperm and egg carries a new mix of the parents' alleles. That's why siblings differ.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH26"},"1AP28":{"d":"AP","tag":"In Evo Terms","q":"Occurs during meiosis when genetic material is exchanged between homologous chromosomes; explains why siblings can be so different","ok":"What is recombination (crossing over)?","wrong":["What is mitosis?","What is linkage?","What is nondisjunction?"],"ex":"Crossing over (recombination) in meiosis swaps pieces between homologous chromosomes, so each sperm and egg carries a new mix of the parents' alleles. That's why siblings differ.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP28"},"1TH27":{"d":"TH","tag":"Gene variants","q":"Variants of a gene, as with brown and blue eye color, are called:","ok":"Alleles","wrong":["Chromosomes","Codons","Gametes"],"ex":"Alleles are different versions of the same gene, such as brown vs. blue eye color.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH27"},"1TH34":{"d":"TH","tag":"Gene expression","q":"An allele that must be inherited in two copies (from mom and dad) to be expressed is:","ok":"Recessive","wrong":["Dominant","Codominant","Linked"],"ex":"A recessive allele only shows when there are two copies (bb); a single dominant allele masks it.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH34"},"1TH31":{"d":"TH","tag":"Genotype 1","q":"If your genotype is homozygous dominant for a trait, which allele combination did you inherit?","ok":"BB","wrong":["Bb","bb"],"ex":"Homozygous means two identical alleles, and dominant alleles are written in capitals: BB.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH31"},"1TH32":{"d":"TH","tag":"Genotype 2","q":"If your genotype is heterozygous, which allele combination did you inherit?","ok":"Bb","wrong":["BB","bb"],"ex":"Heterozygous means two different alleles, one dominant (B) and one recessive (b): Bb.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH32"},"1TH30":{"d":"TH","tag":"Traits and alleles","q":"Near-sighted (N) is dominant; unimpaired vision (n) is recessive. What appears in someone who inherited Nn?","ok":"Near-sighted","wrong":["Unimpaired vision","Partly near-sighted","Far-sighted"],"ex":"One dominant allele is enough.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH30"},"1AP2":{"d":"AP","tag":"The Wacky World of DNA","q":"When both alleles are phenotypically expressed, as in AB blood type","ok":"What is codominance?","wrong":["What is incomplete dominance?","What is pleiotropy?","What is linkage?"],"ex":"Incomplete dominance is a blend (red × white → pink).","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP2"},"1AP23":{"d":"AP","tag":"Cells & Genes, Oh My","q":"A gene with multiple phenotypic outcomes, such as a cat gene that leads to white fur and red eyes","ok":"What is pleiotropy?","wrong":["What is linkage?","What is codominance?","What is epistasis?"],"ex":"Pleiotropy: one gene affects several traits, like white fur and red eyes.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP23"},"1AP24":{"d":"AP","tag":"Cells & Genes, Oh My","q":"Two or more alleles positioned closely on the same chromosome, so they're likely inherited as a unit","ok":"What is linkage?","wrong":["What is pleiotropy?","What is crossing over?","What is independent assortment?"],"ex":"Linked genes sit close together on the same chromosome, so they're usually inherited together.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP24"},"1TH33":{"d":"TH","tag":"Punnett square 1","q":"A homozygous dominant brown mouse mates with a heterozygous brown mouse. What percent of offspring show the brown phenotype?","ok":"100%","wrong":["75%","50%","25%"],"ex":"BB × Bb gives only BB and Bb.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH33"},"1TH35":{"d":"TH","tag":"Brown mouse","q":"A homozygous dominant brown mouse mates with a heterozygous brown mouse. Their allele combinations are:","ok":"BB and Bb","wrong":["Bb and bb","BB and bb","Bb and Bb"],"ex":"Homozygous dominant = BB; heterozygous = Bb. Both mice look brown because B is dominant.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH35"},"1AP21":{"d":"AP","tag":"Cells & Genes, Oh My","q":"Brown coat (B) is dominant over white (b). A BB male mates with a Bb female. The percentage of offspring born with a white coat","ok":"What is 0%?","wrong":["What is 25%?","What is 50%?","What is 75%?"],"ex":"Every offspring gets at least one B.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP21"},"1AC55":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 49","q":"Bird Punnett square (Bb × bb). What will the hatched babies look like?","ok":"50% white feathers; 50% black feathers","wrong":["All white feathers","50% white; 50% a mix of black and white","25% white; 75% black","25% black; 75% white"],"ex":"Bb × bb gives Bb, Bb, bb, bb: half Bb (white, the dominant color here) and half bb (black).","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC55"},"1AC57":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 51","q":"Punnett square for the albino male × heterozygous female. Offspring genotypes:","ok":"Aa, Aa, aa, aa","wrong":["aa, aa, aa, AA","AA, Aa, Aa, aa","AA, AA, aa, aa"],"ex":"The aa male can only pass on a; the Aa female passes on A or a. That gives Aa, Aa, aa, aa.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC57"},"1AC58":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 52","q":"Phenotypes of the offspring of the albino male × heterozygous female:","ok":"50% normal pigmentation; 50% albinism","wrong":["all normal pigmentation","75% normal; 25% albinism","25% normal; 75% albinism","all albinism"],"ex":"Two Aa children have normal pigmentation (A is dominant) and two aa children are albino: 50/50.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC58"},"1AC60":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 54","q":"Phenotypes of the offspring of the two heterozygote parents:","ok":"75% normal pigmentation; 25% albinism","wrong":["all normal pigmentation","25% normal; 75% albinism","50% normal; 50% albinism","all albinism"],"ex":"1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC60"},"1TH38":{"d":"TH","tag":"Punnett square 2","q":"Both parents are heterozygous carriers of Tay-Sachs. Probability a child inherits 2 copies and has the disease?","ok":"25%","wrong":["50%","75%","0%"],"ex":"Tt × Tt gives TT, Tt, Tt and tt. One in four children (25%) is tt and has Tay-Sachs.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH38"},"1AC62":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 55 · 2/3","q":"Tay-Sachs (T/t). Genotype of individuals who CARRY the allele:","ok":"Tt","wrong":["tt","TT"],"ex":"Carriers keep about half of normal enzyme function and show no symptoms.","topic":"Inheritance & genetics","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC62"},"1AC13":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 14","q":"______ is the critical ingredient in the recipe for evolution, generating variation and differences between individuals.","ok":"Mutation","wrong":["Selection","Migration","Replication"],"ex":"Mutation creates new variation; selection then acts on it.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC13"},"1AC14":{"d":"AC","tag":"Question 15","q":"A mutation is","ok":"neutral (neither good nor bad)","wrong":["rarely found in the human genome","always good","always bad","something that never causes problems"],"ex":"Most mutations are neutral. Some are harmful and a few helpful, depending on the environment.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AC14"},"1TH22":{"d":"TH","tag":"Mutation","q":"A mutation that is a single base different from the normal (wild-type) sequence is a","ok":"Point mutation","wrong":["Frameshift mutation","Chromosomal mutation","Nondisjunction"],"ex":"A point mutation changes a single base. Sickle-cell hemoglobin comes from one changed base. A frameshift instead adds or deletes bases and shifts the whole reading frame.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH22"},"1AP22":{"d":"AP","tag":"Cells & Genes, Oh My","q":"Sickled hemoglobin is the result of this type of mutation","ok":"What is a point mutation?","wrong":["What is a frameshift mutation?","What is a chromosomal deletion?","What is nondisjunction?"],"ex":"A point mutation changes a single base. Sickle-cell hemoglobin comes from one changed base. A frameshift instead adds or deletes bases and shifts the whole reading frame.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP22"},"1AP30":{"d":"AP","tag":"May the Force Be With You","q":"The weakest force of evolution but the only source of new genetic variation","ok":"What is mutation?","wrong":["What is natural selection?","What is genetic drift?","What is gene flow?"],"ex":"Mutation is the only source of new alleles, but on its own it's a weak force because new mutations are rare.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP30"},"1TH37":{"d":"TH","tag":"Mutations","q":"Which are TRUE? i. Mutations are how all new alleles first arise · ii. Mutations create new genetic variation in a gene pool · iii. Only mutations in gametes influence evolution · iv. Mutations are a relatively weak force alone","ok":"i, ii, iii, iv","wrong":["i and ii","i, ii, iii","ii and iv"],"ex":"All four are true. Mutation is the only source of new alleles, only mutations in gametes reach offspring, and on its own mutation is a weak force because new mutations are rare.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH37"},"1TH36":{"d":"TH","tag":"Trisomy","q":"Trisomy 18 (Edwards syndrome), with heart problems, feeding difficulties and early mortality, is an example of this:","ok":"A chromosomal abnormality caused by nondisjunction","wrong":["A point mutation","Codominance","Heterozygote advantage"],"ex":"Chromosomes fail to separate in meiosis, so a gamete gets an extra copy.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH36"},"1TH40":{"d":"TH","tag":"Forces of evolution 1","q":"~12,000 reproductively isolated Amish in Lancaster, PA show rising rates of a recessive short-limb, extra-digit trait due to the small gene pool. This is:","ok":"Founder effect","wrong":["Population bottleneck","Natural selection","Gene flow"],"ex":"Founder effect: a small group starts a new, isolated population, so its gene pool carries chance allele frequencies. A rare recessive trait becomes common.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH40"},"1AP31":{"d":"AP","tag":"May the Force Be With You","q":"Dutch colonists in South Africa carried a high share of the Huntington's gene; it became more common among Afrikaners than in the Netherlands","ok":"What is the founder effect?","wrong":["What is a population bottleneck?","What is natural selection?","What is gene flow?"],"ex":"A small group leaves and starts a new population.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP31"},"1TH41":{"d":"TH","tag":"Forces of evolution 2","q":"The Toba supervolcano (70,000+ years ago) reduced humans to a few thousand individuals. This is:","ok":"Population bottleneck","wrong":["Founder effect","Adaptive radiation","Gene flow"],"ex":"A population bottleneck is a sudden, drastic drop in population size, often from a disaster, that wipes out genetic diversity. A founder effect is a small group breaking away to start a new population.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH41"},"1AP34":{"d":"AP","tag":"May the Force Be With You","q":"A zombie apocalypse leaves only the few people in your basement to repopulate the earth","ok":"What is a population bottleneck?","wrong":["What is the founder effect?","What is natural selection?","What is adaptive radiation?"],"ex":"A disaster shrinks an existing population.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP34"},"1TH43":{"d":"TH","tag":"Malaria","q":"In malaria zones, people with one sickling and one normal hemoglobin allele have higher fitness than either homozygote. This is:","ok":"A heterozygote advantage","wrong":["Codominance","A founder effect","A population bottleneck"],"ex":"Carriers with one sickle and one normal allele resist malaria without getting sickle-cell disease, so they out-reproduce both kinds of homozygote. This is heterozygote advantage.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH43"},"1TH42":{"d":"TH","tag":"Dimorphism","q":"Differences between males and females (sexual dimorphism) are best understood as a result of:","ok":"Sexual selection","wrong":["Genetic drift","Artificial selection","Founder effect"],"ex":"Sexual selection, through competition for mates and mate choice, favors traits that differ between the sexes, like larger males or bigger canines.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH42"},"1AP29":{"d":"AP","tag":"In Evo Terms","q":"Male and female differences (sexual dimorphism) are best understood as a result of this special type of natural selection","ok":"What is sexual selection?","wrong":["What is artificial selection?","What is genetic drift?","What is the founder effect?"],"ex":"Sexual selection, through competition for mates and mate choice, favors traits that differ between the sexes, like larger males or bigger canines.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP29"},"1TH39":{"d":"TH","tag":"Modern synthesis","q":"The Modern Evolutionary Synthesis in the 1930s resulted in","ok":"A distinction between microevolution and macroevolution","wrong":["The discovery of DNA's structure","The theory of acquired characteristics","Blending inheritance"],"ex":"Anthro-pardy words it as 'bridging' micro and macro. Know both phrasings.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"TH39"},"1AP25":{"d":"AP","tag":"In Evo Terms","q":"This 1930s movement bridged microevolutionary and macroevolutionary processes, producing a refined definition of evolution","ok":"What is the Modern Evolutionary Synthesis?","wrong":["What is uniformitarianism?","What is the Great Chain of Being?","What is Lamarckism?"],"ex":"The Modern Synthesis (1930s–40s) combined Darwin's natural selection with Mendelian and population genetics.","topic":"Mutation & forces of evolution","sk":"ant1-progress-v2","rid":"AP25"},"2PR0":{"d":"PR","tag":"Species concepts","q":"The biological species concept defines a species as","ok":"A group of populations that can interbreed and produce fertile offspring, reproductively isolated from others","wrong":["A group of organisms that look physically alike","A group of organisms that occupy the same niche","Any population living in one geographic area"],"ex":"The biological species concept: populations that can interbreed and produce fertile offspring, and are reproductively isolated from other groups.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR0"},"2AP21":{"d":"AP","tag":"Macroevolution","q":"This concept defines a species as a group of interbreeding individuals naturally reproductively isolated from other groups (they cannot reproduce, or produce sterile hybrids):","ok":"The Biological Species Concept","wrong":["The Morphological Species Concept","The Ecological Species Concept","The Phylogenetic Species Concept"],"ex":"Defined by reproductive isolation, which is why it can't be applied to fossils.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP21"},"2PR1":{"d":"PR","tag":"Species concepts","q":"Paleontologists naming fossil species must rely on physical form. That's the","ok":"Morphological species concept","wrong":["Biological species concept","Ecological species concept","Phylogenetic tree"],"ex":"You can't test whether fossils interbred, so you go by shape.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR1"},"2PR2":{"d":"PR","tag":"Species concepts","q":"Defining species by the niche they occupy (what they eat, where they live) is the","ok":"Ecological species concept","wrong":["Morphological species concept","Biological species concept","Punctuated equilibrium"],"ex":"The ecological species concept defines a species by its niche: how it makes a living.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR2"},"2PR9":{"d":"PR","tag":"Types of speciation","q":"A subdivision of a population is called a","ok":"Deme","wrong":["Clade","Taxon","Natal group"],"ex":"Allopatric step 1: a barrier splits a population into isolated demes.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR9"},"2TH1":{"d":"TH","tag":"Speciation: squirrels","q":"A large squirrel population is divided by a river. Over time the two sides can no longer interbreed. This is","ok":"Allopatric speciation","wrong":["Sympatric speciation","Peripatric speciation","Stasis"],"ex":"A geographic barrier split a LARGE population (like the Grand Canyon antelope squirrels). A small group at the edge would be peripatric.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH1"},"2AP20":{"d":"AP","tag":"Macroevolution","q":"A group of organisms separated by a land barrier may undergo this common type of speciation:","ok":"Allopatric speciation","wrong":["Sympatric speciation","Peripatric speciation","Anagenesis"],"ex":"Allo- (other) + patr- (homeland): a barrier splits the population.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP20"},"2PR10":{"d":"PR","tag":"Types of speciation","q":"Which is the best primate example of allopatric speciation?","ok":"Bonobos and chimpanzees separated by the Congo River","wrong":["Marbled crayfish cloning","Marmosets giving birth to twins","Lemurs diversifying on Madagascar"],"ex":"The Congo River split the ancestral population into the groups that became bonobos and chimpanzees. A geographic barrier makes it allopatric.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR10"},"2PR8":{"d":"PR","tag":"Types of speciation","q":"A small group at the edge of a species' range becomes isolated and, helped by founder effect and drift, becomes a new species. This is","ok":"Peripatric speciation","wrong":["Allopatric speciation","Sympatric speciation","Anagenesis"],"ex":"Peripatric speciation: a small group at the edge of the range becomes isolated, and founder effect and drift make it diverge quickly.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR8"},"2TH0":{"d":"TH","tag":"Speciation: crayfish","q":"A mutation gave a slough crayfish offspring three copies of each chromosome. She reproduces by cloning and is instantly isolated from the parent species, living in the same place. This is","ok":"Sympatric speciation","wrong":["Allopatric speciation","Peripatric speciation","Anagenesis"],"ex":"No geographic barrier: a genetic change created the reproductive barrier in the same area.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH0"},"2AP24":{"d":"AP","tag":"Macroevolution","q":"This type of speciation involves a spontaneous mutation within an individual, preventing gene flow with the parent population:","ok":"Sympatric speciation","wrong":["Allopatric speciation","Peripatric speciation","Adaptive radiation"],"ex":"Sym- (same) + patr- (homeland): isolation without moving away, like the slough crayfish.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP24"},"2PR4":{"d":"PR","tag":"Pace of change","q":"Slow, steady, continuous change accumulating over long periods is","ok":"Gradualism","wrong":["Punctuated equilibrium","Cladogenesis","Catastrophism"],"ex":"Gradualism is slow, steady change. Punctuated equilibrium is long stasis broken by rapid bursts.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR4"},"2PR3":{"d":"PR","tag":"Pace of change","q":"Long periods of little change interrupted by short bursts of rapid change is","ok":"Punctuated equilibrium","wrong":["Gradualism","Stasis","Anagenesis"],"ex":"Gradualism = slow, steady change over long periods (Darwin's view).","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR3"},"2AP23":{"d":"AP","tag":"Macroevolution","q":"The pace of evolution characterized by long periods of stasis followed by rapid evolutionary change is called","ok":"Punctuated equilibrium","wrong":["Gradualism","Anagenesis","Uniformitarianism"],"ex":"Long stasis, then bursts of change.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP23"},"2PR7":{"d":"PR","tag":"Modes of speciation","q":"A species that stays essentially unchanged over a long period shows","ok":"Stasis","wrong":["Anagenesis","Cladogenesis","Gradualism"],"ex":"Stasis: little or no change over a long period.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR7"},"2AP22":{"d":"AP","tag":"Macroevolution","q":"One pattern of speciation involves species A gradually evolving into species B; this contrasts with species A evolving into two or more different species. The first pattern is","ok":"Anagenesis","wrong":["Cladogenesis","Adaptive radiation","Punctuated equilibrium"],"ex":"Ana-genesis: change along one line, with no split. A splitting into two or more is cladogenesis.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP22"},"2PR11":{"d":"PR","tag":"Extinction","q":"Extinction is","ok":"When no surviving members of a species remain","wrong":["When a species splits into two","When a population becomes isolated","When a species stops changing"],"ex":"The dodo of Mauritius is the course's example of anthropogenic extinction.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR11"},"2TH2":{"d":"TH","tag":"Human influence","q":"______ factors, i.e. human influence, are a major contributor to the endangerment and extinction of other species.","ok":"Anthropogenic","wrong":["Evolutionary","Ontogenetic","Natural"],"ex":"Anthropo = human, -genic = caused by. The dodo is the course's example.","topic":"Macroevolution & speciation","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH2"},"2PR12":{"d":"PR","tag":"Phylogeny","q":"Systematics combines","ok":"Taxonomy and phylogeny","wrong":["Ecology and behavior","Anatomy and genetics","Speciation and extinction"],"ex":"Phylogeny = evolutionary history.","topic":"Phylogeny","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR12"},"2PR13":{"d":"PR","tag":"Phylogeny","q":"A trait shared because it was inherited from a common ancestor is","ok":"A homology","wrong":["An analogy","Convergent evolution","A vestigial structure"],"ex":"A homology is a trait shared through common ancestry. An analogy is a similar trait that evolved separately.","topic":"Phylogeny","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR13"},"2PR14":{"d":"PR","tag":"Phylogeny","q":"Wings in birds, bats and insects do the same job but evolved independently. They are","ok":"Analogies (convergent evolution)","wrong":["Ancestral homologies","Derived homologies","Vestigial structures"],"ex":"Similar function, separate origins.","topic":"Phylogeny","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR14"},"2PR15":{"d":"PR","tag":"Phylogeny","q":"Five digits on each hand (pentadactyly) are shared by primates, frogs, lizards and many other vertebrates. For grouping primates this is","ok":"An ancestral (primitive) homology","wrong":["A derived homology","An analogy","Convergent evolution"],"ex":"Inherited from a distant ancestor, so it doesn't tell primate groups apart.","topic":"Phylogeny","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR15"},"2PR16":{"d":"PR","tag":"Phylogeny","q":"Postorbital closure is shared by all haplorrhines but not strepsirrhines. It is","ok":"A derived homology","wrong":["An ancestral homology","An analogy","A vestigial structure"],"ex":"Ateles (spider monkey) = derived closure; Lemur = ancestral bar.","topic":"Phylogeny","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR16"},"2PR17":{"d":"PR","tag":"Phylogeny","q":"Why was the old “prosimian” group dropped?","ok":"It grouped tarsiers with lemurs on ancestral look-alike traits instead of shared derived traits","wrong":["Prosimians turned out not to be primates","It contained too many species","Tarsiers went extinct"],"ex":"Good groups are defined by shared DERIVED traits.","topic":"Phylogeny","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR17"},"2PR18":{"d":"PR","tag":"Phylogeny","q":"The aye-aye's rodent-like ever-growing incisors are best described as","ok":"Highly derived traits","wrong":["Ancestral primate traits","Traits shared by all strepsirrhines","Homologies with tarsiers"],"ex":"Its dental formula (1.0.1.3 / 1.0.0.3) is the exception to strepsirrhine 2.1.3.3.","topic":"Phylogeny","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR18"},"2PR19":{"d":"PR","tag":"Timing","q":"The Cenozoic, the “age of mammals”, began about","ok":"66 million years ago","wrong":["225 million years ago","6 million years ago","4.5 billion years ago"],"ex":"The Cenozoic began about 66 million years ago, after the dinosaurs died out.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR19"},"2PR20":{"d":"PR","tag":"Timing","q":"After the dinosaurs died out, mammals diversified into many niches (sea lions, elephants, sloths, primates). This is","ok":"Adaptive radiation","wrong":["Anagenesis","Stasis","Founder effect"],"ex":"Adaptive radiation: one ancestral group quickly diversifies into many species that fill open niches. Mammals did this after the dinosaurs died out.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR20"},"2TH3":{"d":"TH","tag":"Protoprimate","q":"The protoprimate emerged approximately ______ years ago.","ok":"66 million","wrong":["6 million","225 million","650 million"],"ex":"Right at the start of the Cenozoic, after the dinosaurs died out. 225 mya is when the first mammals appeared.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH3"},"2TH5":{"d":"TH","tag":"Arboreal heritage","q":"Primates have an arboreal heritage, meaning the first primates had numerous adaptations suited to","ok":"Life in the trees","wrong":["Running on open ground","Swimming","Living in burrows"],"ex":"The first primates lived in trees, so grasping hands and feet, nails and forward-facing eyes are adaptations to life in the trees.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH5"},"2PR21":{"d":"PR","tag":"Why primates","q":"Primates are called “generalized” because they","ok":"Kept flexible features (five digits, mixed diet, varied locomotion) instead of specializing for one niche","wrong":["Are found everywhere on Earth","All eat the same diet","Evolved after humans"],"ex":"The giraffe and seal on the slide are the specialized contrast.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR21"},"2LT0":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q1","q":"David Attenborough (the narrator) explains that a successful life in the trees requires these characteristics:","ok":"Forward-facing (convergent) eyes and prehensile hands","wrong":["Long tails and a cat-like disposition","Colorless fur and laterally positioned eyes","A specialized diet of only leaves and long tails","Claws instead of nails and laterally facing eyes"],"ex":"Judging distance between branches needs overlapping (binocular) vision, and holding on needs grasping hands.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT0"},"2PR22":{"d":"PR","tag":"Primate features","q":"Fingerprint ridges on primate tactile pads are called","ok":"Dermatoglyphs","wrong":["Ischial callosities","Rhinaria","Lophs"],"ex":"Dermatoglyphs are the fingerprint ridges on primate fingers and toes; they help with grip and touch.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR22"},"2AP11":{"d":"AP","tag":"The Essence of Primates","q":"This type of grip, where the pointer finger and thumb can pick up small objects, is found across many primate groups:","ok":"Precision grip","wrong":["Power grip","Hook grip","Prehensile-tail grip"],"ex":"Thumb-to-finger pinching for small objects.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP11"},"2PR23":{"d":"PR","tag":"Primate features","q":"Forward-facing eyes with overlapping fields that give 3D depth perception provide","ok":"Binocular, stereoscopic vision","wrong":["A tapetum lucidum","Color blindness","Olfactory dominance"],"ex":"Crucial for judging distance when leaping between branches.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR23"},"2AP10":{"d":"AP","tag":"The Essence of Primates","q":"In addition to nails and a reduced reliance on smell, this primate structural adaptation allows for binocular and stereoscopic (3D) vision:","ok":"Convergent (forward-facing) orbits","wrong":["A post-orbital bar","A tapetum lucidum","A moist rhinarium"],"ex":"Eyes facing forward overlap their fields of view, giving depth perception.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP10"},"2PR24":{"d":"PR","tag":"Primate features","q":"Among primates, color vision is found in","ok":"Diurnal species","wrong":["Nocturnal species","Strepsirrhines only","Tarsiers only"],"ex":"Color vision helps spot ripe fruit and young leaves in daylight, so diurnal (day-active) primates have it.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR24"},"2LT8":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q9","q":"New World marmosets are diurnal and therefore should rely heavily on","ok":"Visual cues","wrong":["Aquatic resources","Dancing abilities to find mates","Vocalizations","Making tools to access resources"],"ex":"Diurnal primates rely on vision, including color vision.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT8"},"2TH6":{"d":"TH","tag":"Teeth","q":"Having different kinds of teeth (incisors, canines, premolars, molars) that serve different functions is called","ok":"Heterodonty","wrong":["Homodonty","Bilophodonty","A dental comb"],"ex":"Homodont animals, like the boa constrictor on the slide, have teeth that are all the same.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH6"},"2PR25":{"d":"PR","tag":"Primate features","q":"A flat face tucked under the braincase, as in humans, is","ok":"Orthognathic","wrong":["Prognathic","Bilophodont","Heterodont"],"ex":"Prognathic = jutting midface, like a dog's snout.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR25"},"2PR26":{"d":"PR","tag":"Primate features","q":"Encephalization means","ok":"A large brain relative to body size","wrong":["A reduced snout","A long juvenile period","A grasping hand"],"ex":"The expansion is mainly the neocortex.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR26"},"2AP0":{"d":"AP","tag":"How to make a Primate","q":"The most encephalized part of a primate brain is the","ok":"Neocortex","wrong":["Cerebellum","Brain stem","Olfactory bulb"],"ex":"Primates have large brains for their body size, and the expansion is mostly in the neocortex.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP0"},"2PR27":{"d":"PR","tag":"Primate features","q":"Which is a primate life-history trend?","ok":"Single offspring, long gestation and extended ontogeny","wrong":["Large litters and short gestation","Very short juvenile periods","Offspring independent at birth"],"ex":"Primates have few offspring, usually one at a time, long pregnancies and long childhoods (extended ontogeny).","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR27"},"2TH8":{"d":"TH","tag":"Primate ontogeny","q":"Why does a long ontogeny (slow development) matter for primates?","ok":"It gives individuals time to learn social roles and social behavior","wrong":["It lets males grow larger canines","It gives time to learn to dance","It gives time to learn to fight"],"ex":"A long childhood = time for brain growth and social learning.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH8"},"2LT22":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q23","q":"Chimpanzee infants remain dependent and under their mothers' care","ok":"For at least 5 years after birth","wrong":["Until the infant leaves the troop on its own","For only a few days, then the infant is given to another troop","For a few months after birth","For over 20 years after birth"],"ex":"Extended ontogeny: apes have the longest development of non-human primates.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT22"},"2AP2":{"d":"AP","tag":"How to make a Primate","q":"Living (non-captive) primates are found on these continents:","ok":"Central & South America, Africa and Asia","wrong":["Africa, Asia and Europe","Africa, Asia and Australia","Every continent except Antarctica"],"ex":"Wild primates live in the Americas (Central & South), Africa (including Madagascar) and Asia.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP2"},"2TH4":{"d":"TH","tag":"Distribution","q":"Living primates are naturally found in all of the following regions EXCEPT","ok":"Australia","wrong":["Africa","Central and South America","South and Southeast Asia"],"ex":"No wild primates in Australia, Antarctica, or most of Europe and North America.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH4"},"2PR28":{"d":"PR","tag":"Distribution","q":"Lemurs are found only on","ok":"Madagascar","wrong":["Borneo","Sumatra","Mauritius"],"ex":"Lemurs live only on Madagascar.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR28"},"2PR29":{"d":"PR","tag":"Distribution","q":"The only great ape living in Asia is the","ok":"Orangutan (Sumatra & Borneo)","wrong":["Gorilla","Bonobo","Siamang"],"ex":"Gibbons and siamangs are Asian too, but they're lesser apes.","topic":"Primate features & distribution","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR29"},"2TH9":{"d":"TH","tag":"Suborders","q":"The two suborders of primates in the current system are","ok":"Strepsirrhines and haplorrhines","wrong":["Catarrhines and platyrrhines","Cercopithecoids and hominoids","Flying lemurs and tarsiers"],"ex":"Catarrhines/platyrrhines are infraorders; cercopithecoids/hominoids are superfamilies. Flying lemurs (colugos) aren't primates.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH9"},"2CH32":{"d":"CH","tag":"Ranks","q":"Which list is in the correct order, from largest group to smallest?","ok":"Order → Suborder → Infraorder → Superfamily","wrong":["Order → Infraorder → Suborder → Superfamily","Suborder → Order → Superfamily → Infraorder","Superfamily → Infraorder → Suborder → Order"],"ex":"From the largest group to the smallest: Order (Primates) → Suborder → Infraorder → Superfamily.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"CH32"},"2CH31":{"d":"CH","tag":"Ranks","q":"Haplorrhines vs. strepsirrhines is a comparison at which rank?","ok":"Suborder","wrong":["Infraorder","Superfamily","Order"],"ex":"Haplorrhini and Strepsirrhini are the two suborders of the order Primates.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"CH31"},"2CH29":{"d":"CH","tag":"Ranks","q":"Platyrrhines vs. catarrhines is a comparison at which rank?","ok":"Infraorder","wrong":["Suborder","Superfamily","Order"],"ex":"Study guide: suborder = haplorrhines vs strepsirrhines; infraorder = platyrrhines vs catarrhines; superfamily = cercopithecoids vs hominoids.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"CH29"},"2CH30":{"d":"CH","tag":"Ranks","q":"Cercopithecoids vs. hominoids is a comparison at which rank?","ok":"Superfamily","wrong":["Infraorder","Suborder","Order"],"ex":"Cercopithecoidea (Old World monkeys) and Hominoidea (apes) are the two superfamilies inside the catarrhines.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"CH30"},"2CH34":{"d":"CH","tag":"Catarrhines","q":"The infraorder Catarrhini contains which two superfamilies?","ok":"Cercopithecoidea and Hominoidea","wrong":["Lemuriformes and Lorisiformes","Platyrrhini and Tarsiiformes","Strepsirrhini and Haplorrhini"],"ex":"Catarrhini = Cercopithecoidea (Old World monkeys) + Hominoidea (apes).","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"CH34"},"2TH10":{"d":"TH","tag":"Strepsirrhine traits","q":"What is the name for the moist, fleshy pad at the tip of the nose of strepsirrhines?","ok":"Rhinarium","wrong":["Tapetum lucidum","Post-orbital bar","Olfactory pad"],"ex":"The rhinarium = “wet nose” = greater reliance on smell.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH10"},"2AP1":{"d":"AP","tag":"How to make a Primate","q":"Lemurs rely more on their sense of smell than on vision, as indicated by this moist structure at the tip of the nose:","ok":"Rhinarium","wrong":["Tapetum lucidum","Dental comb","Post-orbital bar"],"ex":"The rhinarium is the wet, bare nose tip of strepsirrhines.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP1"},"2TH11":{"d":"TH","tag":"Strep characteristics","q":"Which of the following is NOT a general characteristic of strepsirrhines?","ok":"Well-developed visual acuity","wrong":["2.1.3.3 dental formula","Dental comb","Grooming claw"],"ex":"Sharp, detailed vision is a haplorrhine trait. In NOT questions the wrong answer is usually a trait from the other suborder.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH11"},"2PR36":{"d":"PR","tag":"Strepsirrhines","q":"Which set is ALL strepsirrhine?","ok":"Rhinarium, postorbital bar, tapetum lucidum, grooming claw, dental comb","wrong":["Dry nose, postorbital closure, fused frontal","Bilophodont molars, ischial callosities","Y-5 molars, no tail, brachiation"],"ex":"A rhinarium, post-orbital bar, tapetum lucidum, grooming claw and dental comb are all strepsirrhine traits.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR36"},"2PR37":{"d":"PR","tag":"Haplorrhines","q":"Haplorrhines have a fused frontal bone and a fused mandibular symphysis. Strepsirrhines have","ok":"Unfused frontal and unfused symphysis","wrong":["Fused frontal, unfused symphysis","No frontal bone","A dental comb in the upper jaw"],"ex":"Strepsirrhines keep both the frontal bone and the jaw (mandibular) symphysis unfused; haplorrhines have both fused.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR37"},"2TH13":{"d":"TH","tag":"Lemurs","q":"Lemurs are native to","ok":"Madagascar","wrong":["Southeast Asia","Mauritius","Polynesia"],"ex":"Southeast Asia is lorises; Mauritius is the dodo's island.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH13"},"2LT1":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q2","q":"The locomotor behavior of the ring-tailed lemur of Madagascar (Lemur catta) is characterized by","ok":"Being both terrestrial and arboreal","wrong":["Being aquatic and arboreal","Being exclusively arboreal","Spending most of its time underground in caves","Enjoying how to “move-it, move-it”"],"ex":"Ring-tailed lemurs spend a lot of time on the ground as well as in trees, unusual for a lemur.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT1"},"2LT2":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q3","q":"Ring-tailed lemur males and females have spurs on their wrists and under their tails, surrounded by glands that","ok":"Are important for scent-marking","wrong":["Secrete a sticky liquid that helps them climb trees","Are important for aggressively high-fiving","Secrete toxins to protect their infants from predators","Secrete a toxin that protects tree branches from molding"],"ex":"Strepsirrhines rely heavily on smell. Males also rub scent on their tails for “stink fights”.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT2"},"2LT3":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q4","q":"The sifaka's (Propithecus verreauxi) leaping from tree limb to tree limb is made possible by","ok":"Disproportionately long hind limbs relative to forelimbs","wrong":["Their large, complex brains","Disproportionately long forelimbs relative to hind limbs","Their elongated tails","Their small body size"],"ex":"Long, powerful legs drive vertical clinging and leaping.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT3"},"2LT5":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q6","q":"The smallest of all lemurs is nocturnal and called","ok":"A mouse lemur","wrong":["A pinky lemur","A pygmy lemur","A mini-lemur","A night-watch lemur"],"ex":"Mouse lemurs are the smallest lemurs, tiny nocturnal strepsirrhines from Madagascar.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT5"},"2LT6":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q7","q":"The aye-aye (Daubentonia madagascariensis) reaches grubs and egg yolk in trees by","ok":"Using its elongated, bony third finger as a probe","wrong":["Fashioning twigs into tools to probe into trees","Breaking into trees with the strength of its grasping hands","Using scent-marking to fend off competitors","Using its vision and sense of smell to locate them"],"ex":"One of the aye-aye's highly derived traits, along with ever-growing incisors.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT6"},"2AP8":{"d":"AP","tag":"Guess that Taxa!","q":"A primate that is small, nocturnal, insectivorous and has a dental comb can only belong to this taxonomic group:","ok":"Strepsirrhines","wrong":["Haplorrhines","Platyrrhines (NWM)","Catarrhines"],"ex":"The dental comb is the giveaway: only strepsirrhines have one.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP8"},"2TH14":{"d":"TH","tag":"Prosimian","q":"The old “prosimian” category included strepsirrhines plus this additional group:","ok":"Tarsiers","wrong":["Platyrrhines","Haplorrhines","Hominoids"],"ex":"Tarsiers look prosimian but share derived traits with anthropoids, so today they're haplorrhines.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH14"},"2CH33":{"d":"CH","tag":"Tarsiers","q":"Tarsiers sit in which suborder in the current system?","ok":"Haplorrhines","wrong":["Strepsirrhines","Prosimians","Anthropoids"],"ex":"Old system: prosimians. Current system: haplorrhines (dry nose, partial postorbital closure, no tapetum lucidum).","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"CH33"},"2PR38":{"d":"PR","tag":"Tarsiers","q":"Which tarsier trait is “anthropoid-like” and puts them in the haplorrhines?","ok":"No tapetum lucidum and postorbital closure","wrong":["Grooming claw","Small, nocturnal, vertical clinging and leaping","Insect-eating diet"],"ex":"The grooming claw and nocturnal habits are the strepsirrhine-like ones.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR38"},"2LT7":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q8","q":"The dominant sense of the tarsier (genus Tarsius) is","ok":"Vision","wrong":["Taste","Touch","Hearing","Smell"],"ex":"Each eye is as large as its brain; tarsiers lack a tapetum lucidum, so they rely on sheer eye size at night.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT7"},"2CH13":{"d":"CH","tag":"Where on the chart?","q":"On the Primate Classification chart, “Formerly classified as prosimian” goes under","ok":"Infraorder: Tarsiiformes","wrong":["Infraorder: Lorisiformes","Infraorder: Platyrrhines (NWM)","Superfamily: Hominoidea (apes)"],"ex":"Tarsiers used to be grouped with lemurs and lorises as prosimians; now they are haplorrhines, in Tarsiiformes.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"CH13"},"2CH27":{"d":"CH","tag":"Used twice","q":"“Grooming claw” is used twice on the chart: under strepsirrhines and under","ok":"Infraorder: Tarsiiformes","wrong":["Infraorder: Lorisiformes","Infraorder: Platyrrhines (NWM)","Superfamily: Cercopithecoidea (OWM)"],"ex":"Tarsiers keep this strepsirrhine-like trait even though they're haplorrhines.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"CH27"},"2TH15":{"d":"TH","tag":"Haplorrhines","q":"Which is NOT a typical characteristic of haplorrhines?","ok":"Predominantly nocturnal","wrong":["Larger body size","Larger brain relative to body size","Extended gestation and maturation period"],"ex":"Most haplorrhines are diurnal. Exceptions: tarsiers and owl monkeys.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH15"},"2PR39":{"d":"PR","tag":"Anthropoids","q":"“Anthropoids” are","ok":"Monkeys, apes and humans (haplorrhines minus tarsiers)","wrong":["Strepsirrhines plus tarsiers","Apes only","All primates"],"ex":"Anthropoids are monkeys, apes and humans: every haplorrhine except the tarsiers.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR39"},"2CH9":{"d":"CH","tag":"Where on the chart?","q":"On the Primate Classification chart, ““Dry-nose” primates” goes under","ok":"Suborder: Haplorrhines","wrong":["Suborder: Strepsirrhines","Infraorder: Lorisiformes","Order: Primates (all primates)"],"ex":"Haplorrhines (tarsiers, monkeys and apes) have a dry nose with no rhinarium: the \"dry-nose\" primates.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"CH9"},"2CH11":{"d":"CH","tag":"Where on the chart?","q":"On the Primate Classification chart, “Reduced olfaction compared to strepsirrhines” goes under","ok":"Suborder: Haplorrhines","wrong":["Suborder: Strepsirrhines","Superfamily: Cercopithecoidea (OWM)","Order: Primates (all primates)"],"ex":"Haplorrhines rely more on vision and less on smell than strepsirrhines do.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"CH11"},"2PR40":{"d":"PR","tag":"NWM vs OWM","q":"Broad, sideways-facing nostrils set wide apart indicate a","ok":"Platyrrhine","wrong":["Catarrhine","Cercopithecoid","Hominoid"],"ex":"Platy = flat/broad; cata = downward.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR40"},"2TH16":{"d":"TH","tag":"NWM","q":"Which feature is found among some platyrrhines (New World monkeys)?","ok":"Prehensile tails","wrong":["Post-orbital bar","Tapetum lucidum","2.1.2.3 dentition"],"ex":"Bar and tapetum are strepsirrhine; 2.1.2.3 is catarrhine (NWM are 2.1.3.3).","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH16"},"2LT10":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q11","q":"Howler monkeys of the New World (genus Alouatta), despite their large body size, move well through the forest canopy thanks to their","ok":"Prehensile tails","wrong":["Long hind limbs relative to forelimbs, for leaping between trees","Long forelimbs relative to hind limbs, for swinging from tree to tree","Ability to make loud vocalizations","Ability to build bridges out of tree branches"],"ex":"A grasping tail works as a fifth limb. Only some New World monkeys have one.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT10"},"2AP6":{"d":"AP","tag":"Guess that Taxa!","q":"A primate with a 2.1.3.3 dentition, post-orbital closure and a prehensile tail can only belong to this taxonomic group:","ok":"Platyrrhines (NWM)","wrong":["Cercopithecoids (OWM)","Strepsirrhines","Hominoids (apes)"],"ex":"Closure rules out strepsirrhines; three premolars and a prehensile tail mean New World monkey.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP6"},"2AP9":{"d":"AP","tag":"Guess that Taxa!","q":"A primate with a 2.1.2.3 dentition can only belong to this taxonomic group:","ok":"Catarrhines","wrong":["Platyrrhines (NWM)","Strepsirrhines","Hominoids only"],"ex":"Two premolars = catarrhine: Old World monkeys and apes both have 2.1.2.3, so “hominoids only” is too narrow.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP9"},"2CH35":{"d":"CH","tag":"Dentition","q":"A jaw with three premolars per side points to which groups?","ok":"Platyrrhines or strepsirrhines (2.1.3.3)","wrong":["Catarrhines (2.1.2.3)","Hominoids only","Cercopithecoids only"],"ex":"Count premolars first: 3 = NWM or strepsirrhine, 2 = catarrhine.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"CH35"},"2CH20":{"d":"CH","tag":"Where on the chart?","q":"On the Primate Classification chart, “2.1.2.3 dentition” goes under","ok":"Infraorder: Catarrhines","wrong":["Infraorder: Platyrrhines (NWM)","Suborder: Haplorrhines","Order: Primates (all primates)"],"ex":"Catarrhines (Old World monkeys and apes) have two premolars per quarter: 2.1.2.3.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"CH20"},"2AP14":{"d":"AP","tag":"The Essence of Primates","q":"These high-cusped teeth help cercopithecoids grind up leafy, fibrous foods, and set them apart from hominoid teeth:","ok":"Bilophodont molars","wrong":["Y-5 molars","A dental comb","Homodont teeth"],"ex":"Four cusps in two pairs, each pair joined by a ridge. Apes have Y-5 molars instead.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP14"},"2PR41":{"d":"PR","tag":"NWM vs OWM","q":"Which group never has a prehensile tail?","ok":"Cercopithecoids (Old World monkeys)","wrong":["Howler monkeys","Spider monkeys","Atelids"],"ex":"OWM have tails, but never grasping ones. Apes have no tail at all.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR41"},"2PR42":{"d":"PR","tag":"OWM","q":"Cheek-pouch monkeys (cercopithecines) such as macaques and baboons are","ok":"Omnivorous, storing food in their cheek pouches","wrong":["Leaf specialists with chambered stomachs","Nocturnal insectivores","Strict fruit eaters with throat sacs"],"ex":"Cercopithecines, like macaques and baboons, eat a varied diet and stuff food into their cheek pouches.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR42"},"2PR43":{"d":"PR","tag":"OWM","q":"Langurs, colobus and proboscis monkeys belong to the","ok":"Colobines (leaf-eating monkeys)","wrong":["Cercopithecines (cheek-pouch monkeys)","Platyrrhines","Hylobatids"],"ex":"Colobines (colobus monkeys, langurs, proboscis monkeys) are leaf-eaters with complex stomachs.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR43"},"2TH17":{"d":"TH","tag":"Colobines","q":"This derived feature of colobines lets them digest cellulose-laden plant foods:","ok":"Semi-chambered stomach","wrong":["Cheek pouches","No appendix","Sharp canine teeth"],"ex":"Cheek pouches belong to the OTHER subfamily, the cercopithecines.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH17"},"2PR44":{"d":"PR","tag":"OWM","q":"Which macaque lives outside the tropics, in snowy northern Japan?","ok":"Macaca fuscata","wrong":["Macaca mulatta","Papio anubis","Colobus guereza"],"ex":"Macaques are the most widespread primate genus after humans.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR44"},"2LT11":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q12","q":"Vervet monkeys (Chlorocebus pygerythrus) of Africa spend their time","ok":"Terrestrially","wrong":["Swimming","Living a nocturnal life","In caves","Arboreally"],"ex":"Vervets are Old World monkeys that spend much of their day on the ground, though they climb to feed and sleep.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT11"},"2LT12":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q13","q":"Baboon (genus Papio) diets are made up of","ok":"All of these: fruits, leaves, roots, lizards and rodents","wrong":["Fruits only","Leaves only","Roots only","Lizards and rodents only"],"ex":"Baboons are omnivores, like other cheek-pouch monkeys (cercopithecines).","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT12"},"2LT16":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q17","q":"In general, apes are characterized by","ok":"Lack of a tail","wrong":["Small body size relative to other primates","Larger female body size compared to males","A weak hand and foot grip","Being nocturnal"],"ex":"Apes have no tail. They're generally larger than monkeys, with strong grips, and most are active by day.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT16"},"2TH19":{"d":"TH","tag":"Hominoids","q":"In contrast to bilophodont molars, hominoids have this molar pattern:","ok":"Y-5 molars","wrong":["Dental comb","Brachiated teeth","Ischial callosities"],"ex":"Hominoids (apes) have Y-5 molars: five cusps with Y-shaped grooves. Bilophodont molars belong to Old World monkeys.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH19"},"2TH20":{"d":"TH","tag":"Hominoid locomotion","q":"Moving through the treetops by alternately supporting the body under each forelimb is called","ok":"Brachiation","wrong":["Prehensile tail use","Bilophodonty"],"ex":"Gibbons are the champion brachiators.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH20"},"2TH21":{"d":"TH","tag":"Longest ontogeny","q":"According to this class, which primate taxon has the longest ontogeny?","ok":"Hominoids","wrong":["Platyrrhines","Lorises & galagos","Lemurs"],"ex":"Apes have the longest development (and largest brain relative to body size).","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH21"},"2AP5":{"d":"AP","tag":"Guess that Taxa!","q":"A primate with no tail, a modified scapula and clavicle for brachiating, and Y-5 molars can only belong to this taxonomic group:","ok":"Hominoids (apes)","wrong":["Cercopithecoids (OWM)","Platyrrhines (NWM)","Strepsirrhines"],"ex":"No tail + brachiating shoulders + Y-5 molars = apes.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP5"},"2PR45":{"d":"PR","tag":"Apes","q":"Which ape trait is an upper-body modification for suspensory locomotion?","ok":"Long arms and a scapula on the back with a long clavicle","wrong":["Ischial callosities","Prehensile tail","Bilophodont molars"],"ex":"Wide, shallow chest; shoulders point the arms outward and overhead.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR45"},"2PR46":{"d":"PR","tag":"Apes","q":"Gibbons and siamangs are","ok":"Small (lesser) apes: acrobatic brachiators and frugivores with throat sacs","wrong":["Great apes that knuckle-walk","Old World monkeys","Strepsirrhines"],"ex":"Gibbons and siamangs are the small (lesser) apes: acrobatic brachiators that eat mostly fruit. Siamangs have throat sacs for loud calls.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR46"},"2PR47":{"d":"PR","tag":"Apes","q":"Gorillas are best described as","ok":"Knuckle-walking folivores with marked sexual dimorphism","wrong":["Solitary arboreal frugivores","Small acrobatic brachiators","Nocturnal insectivores"],"ex":"Orangutans are the solitary, arboreal frugivores.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR47"},"2PR48":{"d":"PR","tag":"Apes","q":"Every vertebrate embryo, including humans, has a tail early on. In apes it shrinks to a tailbone. This is evidence of","ok":"A vestigial structure and common ancestry","wrong":["Convergent evolution","An analogy","Punctuated equilibrium"],"ex":"A tailbone is a vestigial tail, left over from tailed ancestors: evidence of common ancestry.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR48"},"2LT17":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q18","q":"Gibbons, a kind of “lesser ape” (genus Hylobates), live an acrobatic lifestyle","ok":"Although there is evidence of many skeletal fractures","wrong":["And are so skilled that they rarely show fractures","Despite having short fingers and arms","Despite having large body sizes","And use their tails to move through the canopy"],"ex":"Brachiating at speed has a cost: many gibbon skeletons show healed fractures from falls.","topic":"Primate taxonomy","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT17"},"2TH23":{"d":"TH","tag":"Field studies","q":"The study of behaviors as they evolved through natural selection within particular environments is","ok":"Behavioral ecology","wrong":["Brachiation studies","Sympatric studies","Fission-fusion"],"ex":"Behavioral ecology studies how behaviors evolved by natural selection in a particular environment.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH23"},"2TH24":{"d":"TH","tag":"Predation","q":"A large primate group may defend against predators through all of the following EXCEPT","ok":"Deletion","wrong":["Detection","Dilution","Deterrence"],"ex":"The three real D's: detection, deterrence, dilution.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH24"},"2PR50":{"d":"PR","tag":"Sociality","q":"More individuals in a group means a lower chance that YOU are the one caught. This is","ok":"Dilution","wrong":["Deterrence","Detection","Dispersal"],"ex":"Detection = more eyes; deterrence = mobbing or scaring a predator off.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR50"},"2PR49":{"d":"PR","tag":"Sociality","q":"Which is a DISADVANTAGE of living in a social group?","ok":"Disease transmission","wrong":["Predator detection","Help with caring for young","Defense of food"],"ex":"Others: competition for food and mates, visibility to predators, social tension.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR49"},"2AP4":{"d":"AP","tag":"How to make a Primate","q":"According to this class, which of these is one of the factors that influence how big a primate social group can get and who is in it?","ok":"Food availability and distribution","wrong":["Whether the species has a prehensile tail","The species' dental formula","Whether the species has nails or claws"],"ex":"The class names food availability and distribution, predation pressure, and dispersal (female vs. male philopatry).","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP4"},"2PR52":{"d":"PR","tag":"Group size","q":"Primates whose food (like insects) is widely scattered tend to live in","ok":"Small groups","wrong":["Large groups","Fission-fusion megagroups","Multi-male troops"],"ex":"Abundant, evenly spread food like leaves supports larger groups.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR52"},"2PR53":{"d":"PR","tag":"Group size","q":"Terrestrial, diurnal primates under high predation pressure tend to live in","ok":"Larger groups","wrong":["Solitary lives","Pairs","Groups of one female and two males"],"ex":"Small, arboreal or nocturnal primates rely on hiding instead.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR53"},"2TH27":{"d":"TH","tag":"Social organization","q":"Based on the relationship between primate size, diet and social behavior, which statement best predicts the social characteristics of a hypothetical primate that weighs no more than a few ounces, with 2.1.3.3 dentition and a tapetum lucidum?","ok":"It is likely a nocturnal strepsirrhine that is insectivorous and spends much of its time solitary in the trees.","wrong":["It is likely a diurnal platyrrhine that is omnivorous and whose species is very sexually dimorphic.","It is likely a nocturnal haplorrhine that is insectivorous and spends much of its time solitary on the ground."],"ex":"The tapetum lucidum points to a strepsirrhine (haplorrhines lack one) active at night. A body of a few ounces needs high-energy food, so insects, not leaves. Tiny nocturnal strepsirrhines, like mouse lemurs and galagos, forage alone in the trees.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH27"},"2PR54":{"d":"PR","tag":"Social organization","q":"“Social organization ≠ mating system” means","ok":"Who lives together is not the same as who mates with whom","wrong":["All primates are monogamous","Group size never affects mating","Mating always happens outside the group"],"ex":"Orangutans live alone but mate polygynously.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR54"},"2PR55":{"d":"PR","tag":"Mating systems","q":"One male mating with multiple females is","ok":"Polygyny","wrong":["Polyandry","Polygynandry","Monogamy"],"ex":"gyn = female.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR55"},"2PR56":{"d":"PR","tag":"Mating systems","q":"One female mating with multiple males is","ok":"Polyandry","wrong":["Polygyny","Polygynandry","Pair bonding"],"ex":"andr = male.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR56"},"2AP17":{"d":"AP","tag":"Primates Behaving… Like Primates","q":"A primate mating system that includes two males and one female caring for offspring:","ok":"Polyandry","wrong":["Polygyny","Monogamy","Polygynandry"],"ex":"Poly- (many) + -andry (males): one female, several males.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP17"},"2PR57":{"d":"PR","tag":"Mating systems","q":"Multiple males and multiple females mating within a group is","ok":"Polygynandry","wrong":["Polygyny","Polyandry","Monogamy"],"ex":"Polygynandry: several males and several females mate within the group, as in chimps and bonobos.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR57"},"2PR58":{"d":"PR","tag":"Mating systems","q":"Which primates live as one female with two adult males and practice cooperative breeding?","ok":"Marmosets and tamarins","wrong":["Gorillas and langurs","Gibbons and owl monkeys","Baboons and chimpanzees"],"ex":"Twins are the rule, so the whole group helps carry and feed infants.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR58"},"2PR59":{"d":"PR","tag":"Mating systems","q":"Gorillas and langurs live in which type of group?","ok":"One male, multiple females (single-male polygyny)","wrong":["Pair bonds","Multi-male, multi-female","One female, multiple males"],"ex":"Gorillas and langurs live in one-male groups with several females: single-male polygyny.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR59"},"2PR61":{"d":"PR","tag":"Fission-fusion","q":"A fission-fusion group is one that","ok":"Splits into smaller foraging parties and comes back together, with shifting membership","wrong":["Never changes membership","Is made of one male and many females","Lives alone except to mate"],"ex":"Chimpanzees (multi-male, multi-female) are the classic example.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR61"},"2AP18":{"d":"AP","tag":"Primates Behaving… Like Primates","q":"Chimpanzees (and other primates) have this foraging structure: a small subset of the community breaks off into a foraging party and, over time, returns to the larger community.","ok":"Fission-fusion","wrong":["A one-male unit","A dominance hierarchy","Pair bonding"],"ex":"Fission (split up) and fusion (come back together).","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP18"},"2TH25":{"d":"TH","tag":"Dispersal","q":"A primate that does not leave its birth (natal) group is","ok":"Philopatric","wrong":["Sympatric","The dominant male","A conspecific"],"ex":"Philopatry = staying in your natal group as an adult.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH25"},"2AP13":{"d":"AP","tag":"The Essence of Primates","q":"Male chimpanzees remain in their natal group. This is another word for staying in one's natal group:","ok":"Philopatry","wrong":["Dispersal","Infanticide","Fission-fusion"],"ex":"Philo- (love) + patr- (homeland).","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP13"},"2PR64":{"d":"PR","tag":"Dimorphism","q":"Sexual dimorphism in body and canine size is mainly the result of","ok":"Sexual selection from male-male competition","wrong":["Genetic drift","Artificial selection","Founder effect"],"ex":"Male-male competition for mates (sexual selection) favors bigger males with bigger canines.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR64"},"2AP19":{"d":"AP","tag":"Primates Behaving… Like Primates","q":"In single-male, multi-female groups, male-male competition for mates is fierce, so this degree of sexual dimorphism is expected:","ok":"High","wrong":["Low","None at all","Females larger than males"],"ex":"Fierce competition among males favors big males with big canines.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP19"},"2TH26":{"d":"TH","tag":"Social & mating system","q":"Pair-bonded primates have little competition for mates. For this reason they generally show","ok":"Low sexual dimorphism","wrong":["High sexual dimorphism","A care-free attitude"],"ex":"With little competition for mates, males don't need to be bigger than females, so pair-bonded species like gibbons show low sexual dimorphism.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH26"},"2PR60":{"d":"PR","tag":"Mating systems","q":"Gibbons and owl monkeys live in pair bonds, but extra-pair copulations are common. Their sexual dimorphism is","ok":"Low","wrong":["High","Extreme in canines only","Reversed (females larger)"],"ex":"Pair bonds mean little competition among males, so males and females are about the same size: low dimorphism.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR60"},"2AP12":{"d":"AP","tag":"The Essence of Primates","q":"The window of time during which primate females are sexually receptive is known as","ok":"Estrus","wrong":["Gestation","Lactation","Weaning"],"ex":"Often advertised by sexual swellings in Old World monkeys and chimpanzees.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP12"},"2TH18":{"d":"TH","tag":"Infanticide","q":"Infanticide as a primate reproductive strategy is based on the idea that the death of a nursing infant","ok":"Accelerates the mother's return to sexual receptivity (she resumes ovulation)","wrong":["Angers females so they defend their territory","Makes females more likely to cooperate with aggressive males","Provides food when resources are low"],"ex":"Top Hat was “select all that apply”, but only this one is correct. The new male can father his own offspring sooner.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH18"},"2PR65":{"d":"PR","tag":"Infanticide","q":"Infanticide is most expected when","ok":"A new male takes over a one-male group with nursing infants","wrong":["Food is abundant","Females are pair-bonded","Infants are weaned"],"ex":"Langurs are the classic example.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR65"},"2AP28":{"d":"AP","tag":"Primate Fitness Regime","q":"The killing of a nursing infant by males (infanticide) serves this function:","ok":"It speeds the mother's return to estrus","wrong":["It lowers competition among siblings","It lets the mother save her energy","It removes sick infants from the group"],"ex":"Nursing suppresses ovulation; without an infant, the mother becomes receptive sooner and the new male can sire her next offspring.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP28"},"2PR66":{"d":"PR","tag":"Dominance","q":"Primate dominance hierarchies are best described as","ok":"Fluid and dynamic: ranks change with age, alliances and health","wrong":["Fixed for life at birth","Found only in males","Always decided by body size alone"],"ex":"They give priority access to food and mates and reduce constant fighting.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR66"},"2PR67":{"d":"PR","tag":"Dominance","q":"A function of a dominance hierarchy is to","ok":"Reduce constant fighting by settling who gets priority access to resources","wrong":["Guarantee equal food for all","Prevent all mating","Replace grooming"],"ex":"Once rank is settled, animals give way instead of fighting over every piece of food or every mate.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR67"},"2AP25":{"d":"AP","tag":"Primate Fitness Regime","q":"Offspring of these females have better survival and growth rates:","ok":"High-ranking females","wrong":["Low-ranking females","Females who left their natal group","The youngest females in the group"],"ex":"High rank brings better access to food and support.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP25"},"2PR69":{"d":"PR","tag":"Displays","q":"A chimpanzee's charging display or a gorilla's chest-beating mainly serves to","ok":"Communicate strength and intimidate without actual fighting","wrong":["Attract prey","Groom other members","Build nests"],"ex":"Displays show strength so that rivals back down without a costly fight.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR69"},"2LT13":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q14","q":"Displays baboons use to threaten conspecifics include","ok":"Eyebrow flashing","wrong":["Urine-marking","No displays; baboons always cooperate","Scent-gland marking","Loud vocalizations"],"ex":"Raising the brows flashes the pale eyelids: a threat that can head off a real fight.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT13"},"2PR70":{"d":"PR","tag":"Grooming","q":"Besides hygiene, primate grooming mainly functions to","ok":"Build and maintain social bonds and alliances, and reduce tension","wrong":["Mark territory with scent","Attract predators away","Establish infanticide"],"ex":"Grooming keeps fur clean, but above all it builds and maintains bonds and alliances and lowers stress.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR70"},"2AP15":{"d":"AP","tag":"Primates Behaving… Like Primates","q":"Many non-human primates spend a huge part of their day doing this, a behavior that acts as social cement, reinforces bonds and lowers stress in both the actor and the recipient:","ok":"Grooming","wrong":["Play","Displays","Foraging"],"ex":"Grooming is hygiene, but above all it builds and maintains relationships.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP15"},"2AP29":{"d":"AP","tag":"Primate Fitness Regime","q":"A behavior that benefits another individual at a cost to oneself is called","ok":"Altruism","wrong":["Kin selection","Philopatry","Infanticide"],"ex":"Kin selection is one explanation for altruism, not the behavior itself.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP29"},"2PR68":{"d":"PR","tag":"Altruism","q":"Helping a relative at a cost to yourself can still spread your genes, because relatives share genes. This is","ok":"Kin selection","wrong":["Infanticide","Philopatry","Sexual selection"],"ex":"Altruism = behavior that benefits others at a cost to the actor.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR68"},"2TH28":{"d":"TH","tag":"Altruism","q":"______ is one explanatory model for understanding why an individual may behave altruistically (e.g., making an alarm call). It argues that even though there may be a cost to the organism's own survival and reproduction, the fitness of the organism's relatives increases.","ok":"kin selection hypothesis","wrong":["dominance hierarchies","display hypothesis","fission fusion hypothesis"],"ex":"Relatives share genes, so helping them can pass on your genes even at a cost to yourself.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH28"},"2PR51":{"d":"PR","tag":"Sociality","q":"Care of young by group members other than the parents is","ok":"Allo-parenting (allo-mothering)","wrong":["Infanticide","Philopatry","Fission-fusion"],"ex":"Allo-parenting (allo = other): group members other than the parents help care for the young.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR51"},"2PR77":{"d":"PR","tag":"Behavior","q":"Members of the same species are called","ok":"Conspecifics","wrong":["Sympatric species","Congeners only","Allopatric populations"],"ex":"Conspecifics are members of the same species (con = with).","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR77"},"2PR72":{"d":"PR","tag":"Learning","q":"Why is the social environment so important for primate learning?","ok":"Long juvenile periods let young learn skills and behaviors by watching group members","wrong":["Primates are born knowing everything they need","Learning only happens alone","Only humans learn socially"],"ex":"Primates have long juvenile periods, so the young learn by watching others in their group.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR72"},"2PR71":{"d":"PR","tag":"Play","q":"Play in young primates matters because it","ok":"Lets them practice physical and social skills they will need as adults","wrong":["Has no function","Only happens in captivity","Replaces learning from adults"],"ex":"Play lets young primates practice movement, fighting and social skills safely.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR71"},"2LT24":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q25","q":"Besides climbing trees, the manual dexterity of chimpanzees (and some other non-human primates) lets them","ok":"Fashion tools, such as sticks for termite fishing","wrong":["Have fist-fights over territorial space","Make phone calls in an emergency","Clasp hands with others to forge alliances","Clap their hands to signal contentment"],"ex":"Jane Goodall's famous finding: chimps make and use tools.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT24"},"2PR73":{"d":"PR","tag":"Tool use","q":"Which pairing of primate and tool use is correct?","ok":"Capuchins: cracking nuts with stones on rock anvils","wrong":["Lemurs: termite fishing with twigs","Gibbons: digging with sticks","Gorillas: cracking nuts with stones in Madagascar"],"ex":"Chimps fish for termites (Jane Goodall's famous finding).","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR73"},"2LT15":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q16","q":"A behavior among the Koshima macaques that can be interpreted as culture (knowledge passed between generations without genetic involvement) is","ok":"Throwing unhusked rice and sand into sea water and skimming the rice off the top to eat","wrong":["Eating sweet potatoes","Living on the beaches of Koshima","Spending time in the hot springs","Picking unhusked rice from sand one grain at a time"],"ex":"An invented technique that spread through the group by learning, like their sweet-potato washing.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT15"},"2AP16":{"d":"AP","tag":"Primates Behaving… Like Primates","q":"According to Life in the Trees, the Koshima macaques throwing unhusked rice and sand into sea water, across many generations, can be interpreted as","ok":"Culture","wrong":["Instinct","Genetic drift","Kin selection"],"ex":"A learned behavior passed on socially across generations.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"AP16"},"2PR74":{"d":"PR","tag":"Culture","q":"In primates, “culture” refers to","ok":"Learned behaviors passed on socially that differ between groups of the same species","wrong":["Instincts shared by every member of a species","Behaviors found only in humans","Physical traits inherited genetically"],"ex":"E.g., Japanese macaques washing sweet potatoes; chimp tool traditions that vary by community.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR74"},"2LT14":{"d":"LT","tag":"Activity 4 · Q15","q":"Besides their dense fur, Japanese macaques (Macaca fuscata) deal with the cold by","ok":"Sitting in hot springs","wrong":["Making fire","Migrating to beaches in the coldest months","Hibernating in the coldest months","Fashioning clothing"],"ex":"The “snow monkeys” are the most northern non-human primate.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"LT14"},"2PR75":{"d":"PR","tag":"Field researchers","q":"Match the researcher to the primate: Jane Goodall studied","ok":"Chimpanzees","wrong":["Gorillas","Orangutans","Baboons"],"ex":"Dian Fossey = gorillas; Biruté Galdikas = orangutans; Washburn & DeVore = baboons.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR75"},"2PR76":{"d":"PR","tag":"Field researchers","q":"Biruté Galdikas is known for studying","ok":"Orangutans","wrong":["Chimpanzees","Gorillas","Japanese macaques"],"ex":"Biruté Galdikas studied orangutans in Borneo. Jane Goodall studied chimps and Dian Fossey gorillas.","topic":"Primate sociality","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"PR76"},"2WS9":{"d":"WS","tag":"Pan","q":"The two species of genus Pan are","ok":"Pan troglodytes (common chimpanzee) and Pan paniscus (bonobo)","wrong":["Pan troglodytes (bonobo) and Pan paniscus (common chimpanzee)","Pan gorilla and Pan pongo","Pan paniscus and Pongo pygmaeus"],"ex":"Chimps and bonobos are the only species the study guide says you need by name.","topic":"Chimps vs. bonobos","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"WS9"},"2WS8":{"d":"WS","tag":"Worksheet Q5","q":"Which geographic barrier separates bonobos from common chimpanzees?","ok":"The Congo River","wrong":["The Nile River","Lake Victoria","The Sahara Desert"],"ex":"Bonobos live only south of the Congo River. A real example of allopatric speciation.","topic":"Chimps vs. bonobos","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"WS8"},"2WS0":{"d":"WS","tag":"Worksheet Q1","q":"How does Richard Wrangham describe chimpanzee social structure?","ok":"Male-dominated: males stay in their natal group, form coalitions, and use aggression to control females","wrong":["Female-dominated through strong female alliances","Solitary, with males and females meeting only to mate","Pair-bonded, with one male and one female raising offspring"],"ex":"Chimp communities are also fission-fusion: they split into shifting foraging parties.","topic":"Chimps vs. bonobos","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"WS0"},"2WS2":{"d":"WS","tag":"Worksheet Q2","q":"How have chimpanzee females responded to the danger of infanticide?","ok":"By mating with many males, so paternity is uncertain","wrong":["By denying sex to all males","By forming female coalitions that dominate males","By leaving the community with their infants"],"ex":"No male gains by killing an infant that could be his own.","topic":"Chimps vs. bonobos","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"WS2"},"2TH22":{"d":"TH","tag":"Chimps and bonobos","q":"According to “Why Sex”, one way common chimpanzee females respond to the danger of infanticide is to","ok":"Mate with all males in the troop to confuse paternity","wrong":["Deny sex to all males in the troop","Deny sex only to males who commit infanticide","Aggressively fight back against males"],"ex":"If any male could be the father, no male gains by killing the infant.","topic":"Chimps vs. bonobos","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"TH22"},"2WS3":{"d":"WS","tag":"Worksheet Q3","q":"How are testes size and sperm quality related to chimpanzee reproductive strategy?","ok":"Females mate with many males, so sperm competes inside the female; males with larger testes and more sperm father more offspring","wrong":["One male controls all mating, so testes stay small","Large testes attract females during courtship","Sperm quality only matters in pair-bonded species"],"ex":"This is sperm competition. Chimps have very large testes for their body size.","topic":"Chimps vs. bonobos","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"WS3"},"2WS4":{"d":"WS","tag":"Worksheet Q3","q":"Gorillas have small testes for their size. Why, compared with chimps?","ok":"One silverback controls the females, so there's little sperm competition","wrong":["Gorillas mate with many partners","Gorillas reproduce asexually","Females choose males by testes size"],"ex":"Gorilla males compete BEFORE mating (huge body dimorphism); chimp males compete after (sperm competition).","topic":"Chimps vs. bonobos","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"WS4"},"2WS5":{"d":"WS","tag":"Worksheet Q4","q":"According to the film, what explains the relatively peaceful relationships between female and male bonobos?","ok":"Females form strong alliances and use sex socially to ease tension, so male aggression doesn't pay","wrong":["Males are much larger and keep order","Bonobos live alone and rarely meet","Males guard a single female each"],"ex":"Female bonobos band together; sex is used to resolve conflict and build bonds.","topic":"Chimps vs. bonobos","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"WS5"},"2WS6":{"d":"WS","tag":"Worksheet Q4","q":"In bonobo society, which sex is dominant?","ok":"Females, through alliances with each other","wrong":["Males, through coalitions of related males","Neither; bonobos have no hierarchy","Whichever sex is larger in that group"],"ex":"Bonobo females form strong alliances with each other, and together they dominate the males.","topic":"Chimps vs. bonobos","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"WS6"},"2WS7":{"d":"WS","tag":"Worksheet Q5","q":"Why are chimps and bonobos so different in social and reproductive behavior?","ok":"Ecology: bonobos have plentiful, evenly spread food and no gorilla competitors, so females can forage together and form alliances","wrong":["Bonobos and chimps are not closely related","Chimps live in captivity more often","Bonobos are nocturnal and chimps are diurnal"],"ex":"Chimp food is patchy and shared with gorillas, so females forage alone or in small groups and can't build strong alliances.","topic":"Chimps vs. bonobos","sk":"ant2-progress-v1","rid":"WS7"}}};
